1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,074
Subtitles downloaded from www.OpenSubtitles.org

2
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:30,520

3
2
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:00,079
That one wants us
to sail them back.

4
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:04,330
That one thinks he can sail
all the way back without us.

5
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:39,010
Yes. East. To the sun.

6
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:42,210
I understand. You can trust me.

7
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,282
To Africa.

8
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:14,809
Well, I have to tack, don't I?
Tack. You'd rather I didn't.

9
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:19,284
You want to get there?
I have to tack.

10
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:04,368
You're going to get us killed.

11
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:15,686
Yea, yea, yea, now go take a bath.

12
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:26,879
We have to signal them.

13
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:32,928
When the ship gets close, he'll run
and hide. Then we both start yelling.

14
00:11:37,480 --> 00:11:40,445
They'll search the ship.

15
00:12:01,720 --> 00:12:04,161
Get ready.

16
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:08,881
Six Weeks

17
00:13:48,040 --> 00:13:50,242
Dolly!

18
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:53,484
Dolly!

19
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:05,648
Carry on, Mr. Packwood!

20
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:07,959
Ave, aye, sir!

21
00:15:07,960 --> 00:15:10,322
Straight ahead!

22
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:37,165
Fire over their heads!

23
00:16:01,640 --> 00:16:02,841
Steady on.

24
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:22,282
Open the gate!

25
00:17:26,600 --> 00:17:29,167
Come on, come on, come on!

26
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:43,691
- Move along!
- Come on, let's go!

27
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:47,242
Move along!

28
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:51,283
- Forward! Come on!
- Keep it going!

29
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:05,522
Come along! Get moving!

30
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:10,199
- Don't stop there! Keep moving!
- Make way for the stinking heathens!

31
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:12,607
All right, move on.

32
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:15,251
Lock 'em up!

33
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:35,560
Gates! Gates! Gates!

34
00:18:40,680 --> 00:18:42,644
Push 'em in!

35
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:45,326
Isabella II, Queen of Spain

36
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:03,648
Your Royal Highness.

37
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:07,009
Something's happened.

38
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:12,799
- Thank you...
- Mr. President, sir.

39
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:14,579
The ambassador to
Spain, Señor Calderon.

40
00:20:14,605 --> 00:20:16,879
Martin Van Buren, 8th President
of the United States

41
00:20:16,880 --> 00:20:18,959
- What?
- Señor Calderon.

42
00:20:18,960 --> 00:20:21,439
- Yes, all right. Not now. Not now.
- Ambassador...

43
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:23,479
It appears to be a matter
of certain importance, sir.

44
00:20:23,480 --> 00:20:24,681
God bless!

45
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:38,159
I'm trying to drink my brandy
after a very long day.

46
00:20:38,160 --> 00:20:41,959
I understand. I simply wasn't
certain whether this was something

47
00:20:41,960 --> 00:20:45,719
- you wanted to take care of personally.
- Leder, there are, what,

48
00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:48,159
three, four million
Negroes in this country?

49
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:52,445
Why on Earth should I concern
myself with these 44?

50
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:54,599
Well, there are reasons.

51
00:20:54,600 --> 00:20:58,168
I don't care how.
You just take care of it.

52
00:21:01,400 --> 00:21:03,762
The ship is the Amistad.

53
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,359
It's too small to be a
transatlantic slaver.

54
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:11,519
So, they're plantation slaves,
then? West Indians?

55
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:16,519
Not necessarily. At least
they certainly don't look it.

56
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:20,088
Not from the glimpse I caught
of them on their way to jail.

57
00:21:20,960 --> 00:21:23,049
They have these scars.

58
00:21:23,960 --> 00:21:25,491
- Scars?
- Yeah.

59
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:46,359
They were first detained by officers of a
survey brig off the coast of Long Island.

60
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:49,799
They were conveyed to New Haven,
under what authority, I don't know,

61
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:52,119
and given over to the
local constabulary.

62
00:21:52,120 --> 00:21:55,927
About 40 of them, including
four or five children.

63
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:06,279
The arraignment is
day after tomorrow.

64
00:22:06,280 --> 00:22:09,239
I can only assume that
the charge is murder.

65
00:22:09,240 --> 00:22:12,879
I'll see what I can do about that.

66
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:18,120
Perhaps a writ for illegal arrest
and detainment to stall things.

67
00:22:18,640 --> 00:22:21,650
At the very least, make sure
they have good counsel.

68
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:05,359
Chief!

69
00:23:05,360 --> 00:23:06,561
Chief! Chief!

70
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:11,207
He's not a chief.

71
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:12,679
Brother!

72
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:14,530
Brother! Brother!

73
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:17,847
He's not your brother.

74
00:23:19,080 --> 00:23:20,850
What is he then?

75
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:23,726
He's a white man.

76
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:25,879
Hear ye, hear ye.

77
00:23:25,880 --> 00:23:28,359
In the matter of the district court
of the United States of America

78
00:23:28,360 --> 00:23:30,839
in this, the year
of our Lord, 1839,

79
00:23:30,840 --> 00:23:34,044
the honorable Andrew T.
Juttson presiding.

80
00:24:00,040 --> 00:24:01,999
If it please Your Honor.

81
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:05,409
The bench recognizes
District Attorney Holabird.

82
00:24:06,840 --> 00:24:11,239
I would like to present the court,
Your Honor, with the charges

83
00:24:11,240 --> 00:24:13,919
- of piracy and murder against the...
- Your Honor, I have a petition

84
00:24:13,920 --> 00:24:16,719
- for a writ of habeas corpus.
- Your Honor, I was speaking.

85
00:24:16,720 --> 00:24:19,639
Yes, I know, Mr. Holabird.
You were reading charges, which,

86
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:21,919
whatever they might be,
will be rendered moot by this writ.

87
00:24:21,920 --> 00:24:25,839
That petition for a writ, Mr. Tappan,
if, indeed, that's what it is, is moot

88
00:24:25,840 --> 00:24:28,439
unless and until an actual writ

89
00:24:28,440 --> 00:24:31,599
by some higher court, by
some miracle, is granted.

90
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:33,559
Mr. Holabird is
correct, Mr. Tappan.

91
00:24:33,560 --> 00:24:36,239
And if you would, sir, while
I know it is your custom,

92
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:39,199
please, kindly refrain from
impersonating a lawyer,

93
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:41,079
which you patently are not.

94
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:42,839
As I was saying, Your Honor...

95
00:24:42,840 --> 00:24:44,201
Your Honor.

96
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:48,639
Mr. Secretary.

97
00:24:48,640 --> 00:24:51,427
Your Honor, I am here on behalf of
the President of the United States,

98
00:24:51,453 --> 00:24:52,905
U.S. Secretary of
State John Forsyth

99
00:24:53,920 --> 00:24:57,599
representing the claims of Her
Majesty, Queen Isabella of Spain,

100
00:24:57,600 --> 00:25:01,679
as concerns our mutual treaty
on the high seas of 1795.

101
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:04,326
- You have my attention, sir.
- Thank you.

102
00:25:04,440 --> 00:25:08,279
These slaves, Your Honor, are by
rights the property of Spain,

103
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:10,559
and as such, under
Article 9 of said treaty

104
00:25:10,560 --> 00:25:12,959
are to be returned posthaste.

105
00:25:12,960 --> 00:25:16,079
Said treaty taking precedence over
all other claims and jurisdictions.

106
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:19,119
Them slaves belong to me
and my mate, Your Majesty.

107
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:20,759
Your Honor, I have
not yet read the...

108
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:22,479
Who be you two gentlemen?

109
00:25:22,480 --> 00:25:25,959
"We, Thomas R. Gedney
and Richard W. Meade,"

110
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:28,679
"whilst commissioned
US Naval officers,"

111
00:25:28,680 --> 00:25:32,799
"stand before this court
as private citizens,"

112
00:25:32,800 --> 00:25:35,039
"and do hereby claim
salvage on the high seas"

113
00:25:35,040 --> 00:25:39,882
"of the Spanish ship La
Amistad and all her cargo."

114
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:42,639
- Here you go, sir.
- Your Honor...

115
00:25:42,640 --> 00:25:47,323
You wish to make this claim above
that of the Queen of Spain?

116
00:25:47,720 --> 00:25:49,959
Where was she, pray, when
we was fightin' the winds

117
00:25:49,960 --> 00:25:52,891
to bring this vessel in,
Your... Honor.

118
00:25:53,120 --> 00:25:57,530
Her Majesty, the Queen of Spain,
was busy ruling a country.

119
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:01,199
Your Honor, these officers'
claims are just...

120
00:26:01,200 --> 00:26:03,846
Your Honor!

121
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:09,291
Here are the true
owners of these slaves.

122
00:26:11,440 --> 00:26:13,599
- Order! Order in this court!
- On their behalf,

123
00:26:13,600 --> 00:26:18,044
I am in possession of a receipt for
purchase executed in Havana, Cuba,

124
00:26:18,200 --> 00:26:21,359
June 26, 1839,

125
00:26:21,360 --> 00:26:26,361
I do hereby call upon this
court to immediately surrender

126
00:26:27,520 --> 00:26:29,210
these goods

127
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:35,079
and that ship out
there to my clients,

128
00:26:35,080 --> 00:26:37,159
- Jose Ruiz...
- Yo soy Ruiz.

129
00:26:37,160 --> 00:26:41,206
"Yoso" Ruiz, and... Pedro Montes?
Pedro Montes.

130
00:26:50,280 --> 00:26:52,519
Mr. Tappan. How do you do, sir?

131
00:26:52,520 --> 00:26:56,279
My name is Roger S.
Baldwin, attorney-at-law.

132
00:26:56,280 --> 00:26:57,519
Real estate attorney?

133
00:26:57,520 --> 00:27:00,999
Real estate, inventories and
other contestable assets, sir.

134
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:02,959
Can I help you with something?

135
00:27:02,960 --> 00:27:05,401
Well, I don't know.
What is it that you do?

136
00:27:06,360 --> 00:27:09,769
Well, I own various
businesses and banks.

137
00:27:10,200 --> 00:27:14,839
Well, as a matter of fact, you
probably could help me, Mr. Tappan.

138
00:27:14,840 --> 00:27:17,999
But that's not why I'm here.
You see, I'd like to help you.

139
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:21,599
- Me?
- Yes. I deal with property, Mr. Tappan.

140
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:23,279
And sometimes I get people's
property back for them,

141
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:24,519
and other times I
get it taken away,

142
00:27:24,520 --> 00:27:26,879
as in this case, which is
clearly a property issue.

143
00:27:26,880 --> 00:27:28,239
You see, all of the claims here,

144
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:30,599
every single one of them, speaks
to the issue of ownership.

145
00:27:30,600 --> 00:27:34,441
- Thank you very much, Mr. Baldwin.
- Baldwin, Roger S. Attorney-at-law.

146
00:27:35,840 --> 00:27:39,319
But I'm afraid what's needed
here is a criminal attorney.

147
00:27:39,320 --> 00:27:42,239
A trial lawyer. But thanks
for your interest.

148
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:44,719
Yes. Well,

149
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:49,879
intending no disrespect, Mr. Tappan,
but if that were the way to go,

150
00:27:49,880 --> 00:27:52,639
well, then, quite frankly,

151
00:27:52,640 --> 00:27:55,286
I wouldn't have bothered coming
down here, would I have?

152
00:28:00,520 --> 00:28:04,008
Goodbye. I bid you gentlemen
a good afternoon.

153
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:07,559
In closing,

154
00:28:07,560 --> 00:28:10,719
I call upon our distinguished
colleague from Massachusetts,

155
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:13,839
Representative John Quincy Adams,

156
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:17,599
to reweigh his unmeet and
unprecedented attempt

157
00:28:17,600 --> 00:28:21,439
to convert this
eccentric bequest of,

158
00:28:21,440 --> 00:28:25,239
let's be frank, a bunch of
junk of one James Smithson,

159
00:28:25,240 --> 00:28:29,570
into a so-called institute
of national treasure!

160
00:28:33,440 --> 00:28:36,371
Perhaps Mr. Adams is
meditating on his response.

161
00:28:48,200 --> 00:28:50,279
Had I thought your
remarks worthy of

162
00:28:50,280 --> 00:28:53,679
any kind of riposte,
Representative Pickney,

163
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:58,442
be assured you'd have
heard from me hours ago.

164
00:28:59,520 --> 00:29:00,999
Who?

165
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,680
Mr. Tappan. Lewis Tappan.

166
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:05,126
Yeah?

167
00:29:06,320 --> 00:29:08,799
I must see him?
I'm required to see him?

168
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:10,839
No, of course not, sir.
He requests an audience.

169
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:12,999
Give me a hand.

170
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:15,319
He requests, does he?

171
00:29:15,320 --> 00:29:19,159
Yeah. I don't know
anyone called Tappan.

172
00:29:19,160 --> 00:29:21,039
Yes, sir, you do. You've met
him on countless occasions.

173
00:29:21,040 --> 00:29:24,159
Where? Where is he?

174
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:27,011
He's right over there, sir.
Right there, sir.

175
00:29:27,680 --> 00:29:30,679
What is that?

176
00:29:30,680 --> 00:29:32,166
- Where?
- Right there, sir.

177
00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:35,279
Lewis! Good to see you again!

178
00:29:35,280 --> 00:29:37,209
And you, sir.

179
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:40,039
- This is Theodore Joadson.
- How do you do, sir?

180
00:29:40,040 --> 00:29:43,687
- An honor to meet you, sir.
- Yeah.

181
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:47,479
- Sir? ls there somewhere we could...
- What? Let's stroll in the garden.

182
00:29:47,480 --> 00:29:49,839
- This way, sir.
- What?

183
00:29:49,840 --> 00:29:52,247
Let go of my arm. Over here.

184
00:29:57,720 --> 00:30:00,479
I believe this case has
great significance, sir.

185
00:30:00,480 --> 00:30:03,199
Our Secretary of State has already
deemed it worthy of his attention.

186
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:05,879
- You don't have to shout. Stop shouting.
- No. Yes.

187
00:30:05,880 --> 00:30:07,919
Well, he's supported
the Queen of Spain

188
00:30:07,920 --> 00:30:10,599
in her claims that the
Africans belong to her.

189
00:30:10,600 --> 00:30:12,759
And then there are others
making claims as well.

190
00:30:12,760 --> 00:30:14,519
- Two sailors...
- What season is it?

191
00:30:14,520 --> 00:30:17,239
- Pardon?
- I said, what season is it?

192
00:30:17,240 --> 00:30:19,159
I don't understand what you mean.

193
00:30:19,160 --> 00:30:23,763
There are two ways of knowing for
certain without consulting a calendar.

194
00:30:23,880 --> 00:30:26,559
The leaves on the maple trees
have all gone and the fact that

195
00:30:26,560 --> 00:30:31,402
the President is not at home over
there on Pennsylvania Avenue.

196
00:30:31,760 --> 00:30:34,879
Tell me, sir, you really think

197
00:30:34,880 --> 00:30:39,919
Van Buren cares about the whims of an
11-year-old girl who wears a tiara?

198
00:30:39,920 --> 00:30:43,919
'Cause I can assure you,
sir, having been over there,

199
00:30:43,920 --> 00:30:46,519
only one thing occupies his
thoughts this time of the year,

200
00:30:46,520 --> 00:30:47,799
being all things to all people,

201
00:30:47,800 --> 00:30:50,039
which, of course, means
be nothing to no one.

202
00:30:50,040 --> 00:30:53,759
In other words, gettin'
himself re-elected.

203
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:55,928
Give me a hand.

204
00:31:03,360 --> 00:31:06,006
- Will you help us, sir?
- Let go of my arm.

205
00:31:06,960 --> 00:31:09,799
Take my stick.

206
00:31:09,800 --> 00:31:12,919
- Mr. Adams?
- Yeah, what?

207
00:31:12,920 --> 00:31:17,759
As an advocate for the abolition
of slavery, will you help us?

208
00:31:17,760 --> 00:31:19,999
I'm neither friend nor foe
of the abolitionist cause.

209
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:21,690
No, I won't help you.

210
00:31:22,240 --> 00:31:24,488
Sir.

211
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:26,199
What?

212
00:31:26,200 --> 00:31:28,399
I know you, Mr. President.

213
00:31:28,400 --> 00:31:30,919
I know you and your
presidency as well as any man

214
00:31:30,920 --> 00:31:32,919
and your father's.

215
00:31:32,920 --> 00:31:36,679
You were a child at his side
when he helped invent America.

216
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:41,119
And you, in turn, have devoted your
life to refining that noble invention.

217
00:31:41,120 --> 00:31:44,439
There remains but one task
undone, one vital task

218
00:31:44,440 --> 00:31:46,799
the founding fathers
left to their sons

219
00:31:46,800 --> 00:31:51,439
before their 13 colonies could
precisely be called United States.

220
00:31:51,440 --> 00:31:56,359
And that task, sir, as you well
know, is crushing slavery.

221
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:59,399
Your record confirms you're
an abolitionist, sir,

222
00:31:59,400 --> 00:32:01,279
even if you won't.

223
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:04,529
- And whether or not you admit it...
- Mr. Joadson.

224
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:08,565
You belong with us.

225
00:32:10,320 --> 00:32:13,679
You're quite the scholar,
Mr. Joadson, aren't ya?

226
00:32:13,680 --> 00:32:15,211
Quite the historian.

227
00:32:19,280 --> 00:32:23,599
Let me tell you something about
that quality, if I might.

228
00:32:23,600 --> 00:32:28,679
Without an accompanying mastery of at
least one-tenth its measure of grace,

229
00:32:28,680 --> 00:32:32,362
such erudition is worthless, sir.

230
00:32:32,720 --> 00:32:35,685
Now, you take it
from one who knows.

231
00:32:37,680 --> 00:32:40,770
If you gentlemen will excuse me.

232
00:32:44,520 --> 00:32:46,239
We know we aimed high coming
to see you, sir, but...

233
00:32:46,240 --> 00:32:48,759
Well, aim lower!

234
00:32:48,760 --> 00:32:52,279
Find yourselves someone
whose inspiration blossoms

235
00:32:52,280 --> 00:32:54,448
the more you lose.

236
00:32:58,800 --> 00:33:03,719
If the court awards them to Spain,
they'll be taken to Cuba and executed.

237
00:33:03,720 --> 00:33:08,439
If the two lieutenants prevail, they'll
most likely sell them to Spain

238
00:33:08,440 --> 00:33:10,559
and they'll be executed.

239
00:33:10,560 --> 00:33:13,199
If Montes and Ruiz are
successful in their campaign...

240
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:17,246
I'm a little confused by something.
What are they worth to you?

241
00:33:17,400 --> 00:33:19,119
We're discussing the
case, not its expense.

242
00:33:19,120 --> 00:33:20,845
The case, of course.

243
00:33:22,160 --> 00:33:26,639
Well, the case is much simpler
than you think, Mr. Tappan.

244
00:33:26,640 --> 00:33:30,447
It's like anything, isn't it? Land,
livestock, heirlooms, what have you.

245
00:33:31,680 --> 00:33:33,211
Livestock.

246
00:33:35,120 --> 00:33:36,919
Yes. Consider,

247
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:41,439
the only way one may sell or purchase
slaves is if they are born slaves,

248
00:33:41,440 --> 00:33:43,961
as on the plantation.

249
00:33:44,960 --> 00:33:47,559
- I'm right, aren't I?
- Yes.

250
00:33:47,560 --> 00:33:50,239
- So, are they?
- "Are they?"

251
00:33:50,240 --> 00:33:53,967
Yes. Born slaves,
as on a plantation.

252
00:33:54,280 --> 00:33:56,839
No, we're not certain,
but we very much doubt it.

253
00:33:56,840 --> 00:34:00,279
Well, let's say they are, and if
they are, then they are possessions,

254
00:34:00,280 --> 00:34:03,319
and no more deserving of a criminal
trial than a bookcase or a plow.

255
00:34:03,320 --> 00:34:04,999
Then we can all go home, can't we?

256
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:06,799
Now, on the other hand,
let's say they aren't slaves.

257
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:09,970
If they aren't slaves, then they were
illegally acquired, weren't they?

258
00:34:10,160 --> 00:34:12,599
Forget mutiny, forget piracy,
forget murder and all the rest.

259
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:15,399
Those are subsequent,
irrelevant occurrences.

260
00:34:15,400 --> 00:34:18,879
Ignore everything but the
pre-eminent issue at hand.

261
00:34:18,880 --> 00:34:22,119
The wrongful transfer
of stolen goods.

262
00:34:22,120 --> 00:34:24,402
- Either way, we win.
- Sir,

263
00:34:25,080 --> 00:34:28,887
this war must be waged on the
battlefield of righteousness.

264
00:34:29,560 --> 00:34:31,279
The what?

265
00:34:31,280 --> 00:34:34,599
It would be against everything I stand
for to let this deteriorate into

266
00:34:34,600 --> 00:34:38,407
an exercise in the
vagaries of legal minutia.

267
00:34:39,480 --> 00:34:41,719
Well, I don't know what you're
talking about, Mr. Tappan,

268
00:34:41,720 --> 00:34:43,239
but I'm talking about the
heart of the matter.

269
00:34:43,240 --> 00:34:44,851
As am I.

270
00:34:45,200 --> 00:34:46,999
It is our destiny

271
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:51,719
as abolitionists and as
Christians to save these people.

272
00:34:51,720 --> 00:34:54,119
These are people, Mr. Baldwin.

273
00:34:54,120 --> 00:34:55,759
Not livestock.

274
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:59,199
Did Christ hire a lawyer to get
him off on technicalities?

275
00:34:59,200 --> 00:35:03,041
He went to the cross, nobly.
You know why?

276
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:06,043
To make a statement.

277
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:09,771
To make a statement, as must we.

278
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:15,200
But Christ lost.

279
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:17,688
- You, I think...
- No, sir, he did not.

280
00:35:18,320 --> 00:35:20,719
Or, at least you, Mr. Joadson,
want to win, don't you?

281
00:35:20,720 --> 00:35:23,039
- Yes.
- I certainly do.

282
00:35:23,040 --> 00:35:26,359
Hell, sometimes I don't
get paid unless I do.

283
00:35:26,360 --> 00:35:30,879
Which brings us back to the
earlier question of worth.

284
00:35:30,880 --> 00:35:32,519
Now, in order to do
a better job than

285
00:35:32,520 --> 00:35:34,199
the attorney who represented
the Son of God,

286
00:35:34,200 --> 00:35:37,244
I'll require $2.50 a day.

287
00:35:49,880 --> 00:35:52,481
Who are they, do you think?

288
00:35:57,560 --> 00:36:01,526
Looks like they're
going to be sick.

289
00:36:09,840 --> 00:36:12,805
They're entertainers.

290
00:36:12,960 --> 00:36:17,609
But why do they look so miserable?

291
00:36:38,360 --> 00:36:40,688
You don't belong here.

292
00:36:40,880 --> 00:36:44,050
Your table doesn't belong here.

293
00:36:51,320 --> 00:36:54,364
This is Temne land.

294
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:02,245
How do you do, sir? How do you do?

295
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:05,684
You want to sit? Sit
over here in Mendeland.

296
00:37:05,760 --> 00:37:08,327
What are you doing, Temne?

297
00:37:08,440 --> 00:37:12,929
You want trouble? Cross
into our territory again.

298
00:37:13,080 --> 00:37:16,559
What is this? Get this
table out of here.

299
00:37:16,560 --> 00:37:18,762
Let me show you to Sherbroland.

300
00:37:30,840 --> 00:37:33,008
There?

301
00:37:59,200 --> 00:38:02,210
I'm sorry. I don't understand.

302
00:38:03,760 --> 00:38:06,359
Excuse me. I...

303
00:38:06,360 --> 00:38:07,719
My name is Roger Baldwin.

304
00:38:07,720 --> 00:38:09,519
This is Theodore Joadson of
the Anti-Slavery Society

305
00:38:09,520 --> 00:38:12,679
and owner of the Forten
Shipping Service.

306
00:38:12,680 --> 00:38:16,680
And this is Professor
Gibbs, a linguist.

307
00:38:18,240 --> 00:38:19,771
What do you want?

308
00:38:20,720 --> 00:38:23,605
Keep talking. Get them to talk.

309
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:26,519
Have you seen this before?

310
00:38:26,520 --> 00:38:30,486
I could kill you with my bare hands before
you raise that sword shoulder high.

311
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:32,559
This belongs to you?

312
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:33,761
Does this...

313
00:38:37,240 --> 00:38:39,488
No. No.

314
00:38:42,560 --> 00:38:45,206
I need to know where you're from.

315
00:38:53,600 --> 00:38:55,484
What did he say?

316
00:38:55,840 --> 00:38:59,599
I didn't understand a word of it.
It's gibberish.

317
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:02,121
- He said, I think, "Show me the map."
- Yes.

318
00:39:02,440 --> 00:39:04,439
What do you think?
Is he here to help us? Or?

319
00:39:04,440 --> 00:39:06,290
I don't know.

320
00:39:07,280 --> 00:39:11,485
He reminds me of that Fula of
Baoma, you know the one...

321
00:39:11,680 --> 00:39:14,839
who hires himself out to scrape
elephant dung from the crop rows.

322
00:39:14,840 --> 00:39:20,524
A dung-scraper might be just the
kind of man we need right now.

323
00:39:20,960 --> 00:39:24,039
Here, Africa, is this
where you're from?

324
00:39:24,040 --> 00:39:26,319
Africa.

325
00:39:26,320 --> 00:39:27,806
What does he want?

326
00:39:27,880 --> 00:39:30,481
He's an idiot. He just likes
to hear himself speak.

327
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:34,039
Were you born in the West Indies?

328
00:39:34,040 --> 00:39:38,325
Were you born in the West Indies?

329
00:39:39,880 --> 00:39:42,845
All three of them are idiots.

330
00:39:43,760 --> 00:39:45,239
What did he say?

331
00:39:45,240 --> 00:39:49,161
He said they have to go away.

332
00:39:54,200 --> 00:39:57,399
- There they are!
- Damnation is God's way!

333
00:39:57,400 --> 00:40:00,959
You cannot own another human being!

334
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:03,439
- Killers of white men!
- Slavery is tyranny!

335
00:40:03,440 --> 00:40:07,565
Look, it's those
miserable-looking people again.

336
00:40:13,560 --> 00:40:15,159
God's blessing on you this morning.

337
00:40:15,160 --> 00:40:18,199
I'm not afraid of you,
or your medicine.

338
00:40:18,200 --> 00:40:23,201
Yes. You place your hand on this
book, and I'll pray for you.

339
00:40:24,880 --> 00:40:27,924
They're human beings, not animals!

340
00:40:28,800 --> 00:40:31,048
Slavery will kill this country!

341
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:38,759
Then, in the quietude of the night,

342
00:40:38,760 --> 00:40:41,039
after the Spaniards
attended their vespers,

343
00:40:41,040 --> 00:40:44,164
and were deep in virtuous sleep,

344
00:40:45,520 --> 00:40:49,039
the savages broke
loose their collars

345
00:40:49,040 --> 00:40:53,839
and stole onto the deck
like creatures of prey,

346
00:40:53,840 --> 00:40:57,647
where they fell upon the unsuspecting
crew with these sabers and cane knives.

347
00:40:58,800 --> 00:41:02,199
I cannot overstate the
inhumanity of their acts...

348
00:41:02,200 --> 00:41:04,599
Have you figured out who he is?

349
00:41:04,600 --> 00:41:05,879
The savagery, the bloodshed.

350
00:41:05,880 --> 00:41:07,679
An advisor of some kind.
Maybe theirs.

351
00:41:07,680 --> 00:41:09,719
Unsated by the mere
deaths of their victims,

352
00:41:09,720 --> 00:41:11,879
they went on to mutilate
at least one of them...

353
00:41:11,880 --> 00:41:13,879
What about him?

354
00:41:13,880 --> 00:41:16,199
To dismember him.
The simple cook, a Creole.

355
00:41:16,200 --> 00:41:17,279
Their own kind.

356
00:41:17,280 --> 00:41:19,119
Theirs maybe.

357
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:21,639
But for the bravery of
Señors Ruiz and Montes,

358
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:23,079
who fought their way to the stern

359
00:41:23,080 --> 00:41:25,159
and steered the Amistad
to these shores,

360
00:41:25,160 --> 00:41:27,359
under constant threat of like fate,

361
00:41:27,360 --> 00:41:29,519
we might never have
heard of this massacre,

362
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:30,919
this bloodbath.

363
00:41:30,920 --> 00:41:32,759
But for their bravery,

364
00:41:32,760 --> 00:41:36,159
these villains most certainly
would have escaped justice.

365
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:39,204
But they've not. They've not.

366
00:41:43,560 --> 00:41:46,764
And Dung-Scraper?

367
00:41:46,840 --> 00:41:51,409
I have a horrible feeling
he talks for us.

368
00:42:05,120 --> 00:42:09,245
Do you know the difference
between a cow and a cabbage?

369
00:42:10,360 --> 00:42:13,319
A brick and a bear?

370
00:42:13,320 --> 00:42:18,287
Or how about a polecat
and a President?

371
00:42:20,920 --> 00:42:22,079
Well, the Spanish government hopes

372
00:42:22,080 --> 00:42:23,959
you don't have a whole lot
more common sense than that.

373
00:42:23,960 --> 00:42:25,279
And I'll tell you why.

374
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:28,639
This case isn't about murder,
mayhem or massacres.

375
00:42:28,640 --> 00:42:31,047
It's not about anything
that dramatic.

376
00:42:31,240 --> 00:42:36,765
This case is about knowing the
difference between here and there.

377
00:42:40,840 --> 00:42:43,168
I want to show you something.

378
00:42:53,320 --> 00:42:55,045
Open your mouth.

379
00:42:55,440 --> 00:42:56,679
Of course, he doesn't
understand what I'm saying

380
00:42:56,680 --> 00:42:58,291
because he doesn't speak English.

381
00:43:02,400 --> 00:43:03,959
Doesn't he understand that?

382
00:43:03,960 --> 00:43:05,519
I thought he was born
on a Cuban plantation.

383
00:43:05,520 --> 00:43:07,279
That's what they're all saying.

384
00:43:07,280 --> 00:43:09,559
Perhaps he simply just doesn't
like you, Mr. Baldwin.

385
00:43:09,560 --> 00:43:12,047
He wouldn't be the
first, Mr. Holabird.

386
00:43:23,560 --> 00:43:25,119
Yes.

387
00:43:25,120 --> 00:43:29,041
What, did he learn this on some Cuban
plantation, this decorative effect?

388
00:43:34,720 --> 00:43:36,888
Cuban plantation.

389
00:43:38,880 --> 00:43:41,606
Stand up. Stand up.

390
00:43:41,680 --> 00:43:43,450
Hey.

391
00:44:02,160 --> 00:44:04,522
Stand up.

392
00:44:07,080 --> 00:44:08,611
Thank you.

393
00:44:09,120 --> 00:44:11,999
Your Honor, I speak more Spanish,
and I was born in Philadelphia.

394
00:44:12,000 --> 00:44:15,919
Your Honor, on Spanish plantations,
slaves always choose to live

395
00:44:15,920 --> 00:44:19,199
surrounded by their own
ways and simple languages.

396
00:44:19,200 --> 00:44:23,999
Pray tell, what need they know of
Spanish? Fetch? Carry? Stop?

397
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:29,079
Ofttimes, gestures suffice for slaves, as,
indeed, for any other beast of burden.

398
00:44:29,080 --> 00:44:30,770
Your Honor.

399
00:44:31,640 --> 00:44:35,287
I represent the interests
of Señors Ruiz and Montes.

400
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:37,079
I remember.

401
00:44:37,080 --> 00:44:41,439
I have a bill of sale issued in
Havana for the purchase of slaves.

402
00:44:41,440 --> 00:44:43,599
I remember that, too.

403
00:44:43,600 --> 00:44:47,919
On it, in addition to the amounts
paid for each, are their names.

404
00:44:47,920 --> 00:44:52,409
Jose, Bernardo, Paco and so on.

405
00:44:53,680 --> 00:44:57,680
On behalf of my clients, I submit
this document to the court.

406
00:45:05,880 --> 00:45:08,959
Mr. Baldwin, you've proffered
to this court this morning

407
00:45:08,960 --> 00:45:12,801
a good deal of, I'll be kind,
circumstantial evidence.

408
00:45:12,880 --> 00:45:17,479
Have you, in addition, anything, say,
in the order of actual documentation

409
00:45:17,480 --> 00:45:19,319
that might refute this one,

410
00:45:19,320 --> 00:45:22,479
and in so doing, more
compellingly support your claims?

411
00:45:22,480 --> 00:45:26,199
I'm sure I could manufacture some
as easily as they have, Your Honor,

412
00:45:26,200 --> 00:45:27,359
if that would suffice.

413
00:45:27,360 --> 00:45:29,639
But what you're saying
is, then, you don't.

414
00:45:29,640 --> 00:45:31,808
Is that correct?

415
00:45:40,280 --> 00:45:42,289
I have them.

416
00:45:42,880 --> 00:45:45,560
I'm afraid that does
not impress me.

417
00:45:56,680 --> 00:45:58,211
I thought you did quite well.

418
00:45:59,760 --> 00:46:01,159
You do?

419
00:46:01,160 --> 00:46:04,919
Much better than I
expected, to be honest.

420
00:46:04,920 --> 00:46:06,799
Well, thank you, I think.

421
00:46:06,800 --> 00:46:09,599
Although, I was concerned for a
moment that you might've forgotten

422
00:46:09,600 --> 00:46:11,564
that this is just a
case like any other.

423
00:46:11,600 --> 00:46:13,450
Well, you needn't worry about that.

424
00:46:13,840 --> 00:46:15,644
That's good.

425
00:46:34,920 --> 00:46:37,839
Hello, Cinque.
My name is Roger Baldwin.

426
00:46:37,840 --> 00:46:40,168
I'll be your attorney.

427
00:46:55,400 --> 00:46:58,444
Yes. Thank you.

428
00:47:07,920 --> 00:47:10,088
All right.

429
00:47:14,200 --> 00:47:17,199
I need to prove where you're from.

430
00:47:17,200 --> 00:47:20,768
You want to show them
where we're from.

431
00:47:23,680 --> 00:47:26,611
How are you supposed
to tell me? I...

432
00:47:28,920 --> 00:47:33,125
How can I explain to
you where we're from?

433
00:47:37,520 --> 00:47:38,599
Maybe, I...

434
00:47:38,600 --> 00:47:39,759
Listen, why don't I... You go.

435
00:47:39,760 --> 00:47:44,363
I'm sorry, you should talk first.

436
00:47:58,000 --> 00:48:01,439
Here. This is where I'm from.

437
00:48:01,440 --> 00:48:03,439
Here. This.

438
00:48:03,440 --> 00:48:07,919
All of this. All of this is...

439
00:48:07,920 --> 00:48:09,279
That's here.

440
00:48:09,280 --> 00:48:10,891
My home.

441
00:48:11,240 --> 00:48:12,566
Here.

442
00:48:20,320 --> 00:48:22,966
Cuba. Cuba's an island.

443
00:48:34,160 --> 00:48:37,319
The Amistad. This is where you...

444
00:48:37,320 --> 00:48:41,286
Where everyone was killed. Here.

445
00:48:42,040 --> 00:48:43,999
Cuba, the Amistad.

446
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:47,602
Now, Cuba, is this your home?
I don't think so.

447
00:48:48,040 --> 00:48:50,481
But... Excuse me.

448
00:48:53,520 --> 00:48:55,722
Now...

449
00:49:01,600 --> 00:49:03,239
Africa.

450
00:49:03,240 --> 00:49:06,319
This is your home, isn't it?

451
00:49:06,320 --> 00:49:11,731
This is your home. Yes?

452
00:49:12,520 --> 00:49:18,602
You came all the way from here.

453
00:49:46,560 --> 00:49:48,279
This...

454
00:49:48,280 --> 00:49:51,609
This... ls how far I've come.

455
00:50:15,840 --> 00:50:18,361
Secure the barrels for transport.

456
00:50:23,960 --> 00:50:26,322
Put your backs into it,
boys. Come on.

457
00:50:26,480 --> 00:50:28,359
This is a court order granting
us permission to board

458
00:50:28,360 --> 00:50:31,199
and search this vessel
for evidential purposes.

459
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:32,519
What did they want?

460
00:50:32,520 --> 00:50:33,719
To come aboard.

461
00:50:33,720 --> 00:50:38,130
I informed them they needed to obtain
one of these, an authentic one.

462
00:50:46,640 --> 00:50:48,763
Aren't you coming?

463
00:51:09,440 --> 00:51:11,722
Heave-ho!

464
00:53:07,440 --> 00:53:09,722
The light. Light...

465
00:53:11,160 --> 00:53:13,169
Light the lamp, Mr. Baldwin.

466
00:53:13,240 --> 00:53:15,363
I'm trying.

467
00:53:21,600 --> 00:53:23,959
Mr. Joadson!

468
00:53:23,960 --> 00:53:26,128
Here.

469
00:53:30,240 --> 00:53:32,363
You all right?

470
00:53:36,120 --> 00:53:37,606
Yes.

471
00:54:17,720 --> 00:54:20,079
These papers,

472
00:54:20,080 --> 00:54:23,599
and I shall ask you to examine them,
are portions of a ship's manifest

473
00:54:23,600 --> 00:54:26,639
I retrieved from the
Amistad yesterday eve.

474
00:54:26,640 --> 00:54:27,799
At first glance, perhaps,

475
00:54:27,800 --> 00:54:31,368
they may appear to bolster
the prosecution's case.

476
00:54:32,120 --> 00:54:34,919
You see, they list cargo,

477
00:54:34,920 --> 00:54:37,359
cargo bearing the
very Spanish names

478
00:54:37,360 --> 00:54:41,690
that Messrs. Ruiz and Montes
insist represent my clients,

479
00:54:42,520 --> 00:54:45,639
hand-scrawled in the margins.

480
00:54:45,640 --> 00:54:50,129
But, no, this is not the manifest
of the Amistad at all. Look.

481
00:54:51,840 --> 00:54:55,799
This is part of the cargo
manifest of a Portuguese vessel,

482
00:54:55,800 --> 00:54:59,799
the notorious transatlantic
slave ship, the Tecora.

483
00:54:59,800 --> 00:55:01,889
The Tecora.

484
00:55:04,200 --> 00:55:07,599
And I can bring you as many witnesses
as you wish, Mr. Holabird,

485
00:55:07,600 --> 00:55:09,239
- to corroborate this.
- Tecora.

486
00:55:09,240 --> 00:55:11,519
Their clients trade primarily
off the coast of West Africa,

487
00:55:11,520 --> 00:55:13,848
the Ivory Coast, Sierra Leone.

488
00:55:16,280 --> 00:55:18,399
I know what you're thinking.

489
00:55:18,400 --> 00:55:20,959
Sierra Leone is a protectorate
of the British Crown.

490
00:55:20,960 --> 00:55:23,199
Slavery is outlawed there.

491
00:55:23,200 --> 00:55:26,802
Its principal port, in fact, has
been rechristened "Freetown."

492
00:55:27,480 --> 00:55:30,399
How then, can the
Portuguese-flag-flying Tecora

493
00:55:30,400 --> 00:55:33,479
engage in the slave
trades in these waters?

494
00:55:33,480 --> 00:55:36,570
I'll tell you how, in a word.

495
00:55:39,240 --> 00:55:41,283
Illegally.

496
00:55:43,520 --> 00:55:46,319
Now, whatever these men say from
this point on clearly matters not

497
00:55:46,320 --> 00:55:49,079
because this proves them liars.

498
00:55:49,080 --> 00:55:51,479
My clients' journey did
not begin in Havana,

499
00:55:51,480 --> 00:55:54,639
as they claim and keep claiming
more and more emphatically.

500
00:55:54,640 --> 00:55:56,968
No, my clients' journey

501
00:56:00,480 --> 00:56:03,206
began much, much further away.

502
00:56:16,480 --> 00:56:19,479
I underestimated you, sir!

503
00:56:19,480 --> 00:56:21,239
I really did!

504
00:56:21,240 --> 00:56:23,279
Well, I should take
that as a compliment.

505
00:56:23,280 --> 00:56:25,079
You should, indeed, sir.
You should, indeed.

506
00:56:25,080 --> 00:56:26,479
You've done it, I think.

507
00:56:26,480 --> 00:56:30,241
I can't imagine there's no not
reaching a favorable conclusion...

508
00:56:30,640 --> 00:56:32,808
Hey! Hey!

509
00:56:34,240 --> 00:56:36,363
Am I bleeding?

510
00:56:37,960 --> 00:56:40,367
There's nothing there.

511
00:56:42,000 --> 00:56:44,639
What did I do to deserve this?

512
00:56:44,640 --> 00:56:47,127
You took the case, Mr. Baldwin.

513
00:56:47,880 --> 00:56:50,208
You took the case, sir.

514
00:56:50,520 --> 00:56:53,007
I should take that as a compliment.

515
00:56:53,800 --> 00:56:56,002
"As you may perceive,"

516
00:56:56,360 --> 00:57:00,279
"I wish you to act promptly before
this matter of the Africans"

517
00:57:00,280 --> 00:57:02,519
"becomes a weight on
our two countries."

518
00:57:02,520 --> 00:57:04,599
"Great countries."

519
00:57:04,600 --> 00:57:07,079
"Our great countries."

520
00:57:07,080 --> 00:57:10,841
"After all, the business of great
countries is to do business."

521
00:57:12,720 --> 00:57:16,129
"Slavery is our pillar of
commerce in the New World."

522
00:57:16,320 --> 00:57:20,559
"Without it, our goodwill and
excellent trade relations..."

523
00:57:20,560 --> 00:57:24,162
- "Should be imperiled."
- "Should be imperiled.

524
00:57:24,560 --> 00:57:27,359
"Without it, we might have
been denied the glory"

525
00:57:27,360 --> 00:57:30,882
"of aiding you in your virtuous
rebellion against the British."

526
00:57:31,200 --> 00:57:35,724
"As slave-owning nations,
we must together stand firm."

527
00:57:36,800 --> 00:57:40,439
"Speak the words of humaneness
for the masses of your citizens,"

528
00:57:40,440 --> 00:57:44,359
"but hold tightly to the
power that protects them."

529
00:57:44,360 --> 00:57:47,199
"That power, of course,
is their wealth."

530
00:57:47,200 --> 00:57:50,449
"The Africans must never go free."

531
00:57:50,800 --> 00:57:53,399
"With sincerest admiration,"

532
00:57:53,400 --> 00:57:57,127
"Isabella II, Queen
of all of Spain."

533
00:57:57,240 --> 00:58:02,159
I am not about to bend to the
will of some pubescent queen.

534
00:58:02,160 --> 00:58:05,399
Forget about them.
They're unimportant.

535
00:58:05,400 --> 00:58:07,879
- Prepubescent.
- What you need to concern yourself with

536
00:58:07,880 --> 00:58:11,079
is what this matter means
here, not an ocean away.

537
00:58:11,080 --> 00:58:13,282
I wish someone would
tell me what it means!

538
00:58:13,560 --> 00:58:16,479
Leder, you yourself said
it was meaningless.

539
00:58:16,480 --> 00:58:18,284
Well, not anymore.

540
00:58:21,360 --> 00:58:23,519
John Calhoun paid me a visit.

541
00:58:23,520 --> 00:58:24,919
Oh, God.

542
00:58:24,920 --> 00:58:29,039
To explain to me why this case
is of much greater import,

543
00:58:29,040 --> 00:58:31,919
much greater symbolism,
to use his word,

544
00:58:31,920 --> 00:58:33,690
to the South and the North.

545
00:58:34,560 --> 00:58:37,279
Now, if the Africans are executed,

546
00:58:37,280 --> 00:58:39,279
the abolitionists will
certainly make good use of it.

547
00:58:39,280 --> 00:58:41,719
And, yes, they will
make some converts.

548
00:58:41,720 --> 00:58:45,679
If, on the other hand, they
are freed, if that happens,

549
00:58:45,680 --> 00:58:47,679
Calhoun assures me the
Southern states will

550
00:58:47,680 --> 00:58:51,362
so ally themselves against you, that
you can forget about re-election.

551
00:58:52,720 --> 00:58:54,279
Over this?

552
00:58:54,280 --> 00:58:55,679
It's worse than that.

553
00:58:55,680 --> 00:58:58,159
Worse? What could
be worse than that?

554
00:58:58,160 --> 00:59:01,439
Calhoun believes, and I am not sure
I can entirely disagree with him,

555
00:59:01,440 --> 00:59:05,486
that this could take us all one
long step closer to civil war.

556
00:59:06,960 --> 00:59:08,239
This?

557
00:59:08,240 --> 00:59:10,169
Yes, Mr. President.

558
00:59:10,520 --> 00:59:13,119
But all is not lost.

559
00:59:13,120 --> 00:59:15,359
Yes, the jury appears disposed
towards freeing them.

560
00:59:15,360 --> 00:59:17,005
But juries can be dismissed.

561
00:59:19,720 --> 00:59:21,160
They can?

562
00:59:22,720 --> 00:59:26,083
But I believe we must go
further and remove the judge.

563
00:59:26,360 --> 00:59:28,959
We can do that?

564
00:59:28,960 --> 00:59:33,609
He could be prevailed upon to recuse
himself for any number of reasons.

565
00:59:33,880 --> 00:59:35,159
And with that in mind,

566
00:59:35,160 --> 00:59:38,479
I've taken the liberty of
exploring possible replacements,

567
00:59:38,480 --> 00:59:41,889
and I've found one I strongly
believe to be better.

568
00:59:43,440 --> 00:59:47,879
He's young, which means he has a
career before him rather than behind,

569
00:59:47,880 --> 00:59:50,119
which means he has yet
to feel the hankering

570
00:59:50,120 --> 00:59:53,399
for magnanimous last gestures
for the sake of posterity.

571
00:59:53,400 --> 00:59:59,369
And he is monumentally insecure,
particularly about his Catholic heritage.

572
00:59:59,800 --> 01:00:02,759
- He's Catholic?
- His grandfather was Catholic,

573
01:00:02,760 --> 01:00:07,359
which young Mr. Coglin has striven
all his days to keep quiet.

574
01:00:07,360 --> 01:00:09,719
Mr. President, meet Judge Coglin.

575
01:00:09,720 --> 01:00:12,799
Judge Coglin, we are so
pleased to meet you.

576
01:00:12,800 --> 01:00:14,729
Thank you so much for coming.

577
01:00:36,200 --> 01:00:38,767
I've been reading in the papers

578
01:00:38,880 --> 01:00:43,608
the continuing saga of the Amistad.

579
01:00:44,440 --> 01:00:45,687
Real papers.

580
01:00:46,680 --> 01:00:49,486
Real papers. Yes, sir.

581
01:00:51,080 --> 01:00:54,966
Yes. Bad luck, this
last unfolding chapter.

582
01:00:55,680 --> 01:00:57,719
What to do now?

583
01:00:57,720 --> 01:01:01,129
Which is precisely why I came to
Massachusetts and imposed on you, sir.

584
01:01:01,400 --> 01:01:04,729
No imposition, really.

585
01:01:05,920 --> 01:01:08,282
Yeah.

586
01:01:10,520 --> 01:01:13,079
How did that...

587
01:01:13,080 --> 01:01:16,119
How did that young
lawyer take the news?

588
01:01:16,120 --> 01:01:19,210
In stride, sir. In stride.

589
01:01:19,960 --> 01:01:23,559
The thing is, sir,
he did everything right.

590
01:01:23,560 --> 01:01:25,839
- He proved the case.
- Did he?

591
01:01:25,840 --> 01:01:28,771
Yes, sir. Surprisingly, he did.

592
01:01:30,400 --> 01:01:31,599
Good.

593
01:01:31,600 --> 01:01:34,599
Well, he'll just have to do
it again, then, won't he?

594
01:01:34,600 --> 01:01:37,485
But like most things, it should
be easier the second time around.

595
01:01:39,240 --> 01:01:43,039
Well, I'm afraid it doesn't
matter what he does now, sir.

596
01:01:43,040 --> 01:01:46,722
Rumor has it our next judge was
handpicked by Van Buren himself.

597
01:01:47,520 --> 01:01:49,290
No!

598
01:01:50,920 --> 01:01:55,199
Well, I'm embarrassed to admit
that I was under the misconception

599
01:01:55,200 --> 01:01:58,688
that our executive and judicial
branches were separate.

600
01:01:59,120 --> 01:02:02,324
No more so than these, Mr. Joadson.

601
01:02:02,880 --> 01:02:05,606
No more so than these.

602
01:02:07,320 --> 01:02:08,679
So now you know.

603
01:02:08,680 --> 01:02:10,759
Yes.

604
01:02:10,760 --> 01:02:12,399
- Sir?
- Yeah?

605
01:02:12,400 --> 01:02:15,639
Mr. President, if it was
you handling the case...

606
01:02:15,640 --> 01:02:18,650
But it isn't me.
And thank God for that.

607
01:02:21,160 --> 01:02:24,399
But if it was, sir,

608
01:02:24,400 --> 01:02:26,682
what would you do?

609
01:02:34,160 --> 01:02:40,766
Well, when I was an attorney
a long time ago, young man,

610
01:02:41,120 --> 01:02:45,723
I realized after
much trial and error

611
01:02:46,000 --> 01:02:47,611
that in a courtroom

612
01:02:48,840 --> 01:02:52,408
whoever tells the best story wins.

613
01:02:53,880 --> 01:02:59,644
In unlawyer-like fashion, I give you
that scrap of wisdom free of charge.

614
01:03:03,360 --> 01:03:06,086
I'm much obliged for
your time, sir.

615
01:03:08,480 --> 01:03:11,604
What is their story, by the way?

616
01:03:13,040 --> 01:03:14,480
Sir?

617
01:03:15,880 --> 01:03:17,809
What is their story?

618
01:03:19,560 --> 01:03:22,445
Why, they're...

619
01:03:24,720 --> 01:03:26,843
They're from West Africa.

620
01:03:27,080 --> 01:03:31,251
No. What is their story?

621
01:03:38,880 --> 01:03:43,244
Mr. Joadson, you're
from where originally?

622
01:03:44,000 --> 01:03:46,159
Why, Georgia, sir.

623
01:03:46,160 --> 01:03:48,519
- Georgia.
- Yes, sir.

624
01:03:48,520 --> 01:03:50,719
Does that pretty much
sum up what you are?

625
01:03:50,720 --> 01:03:54,049
A Georgian? Is that your story?

626
01:03:55,080 --> 01:03:59,479
No. You're an ex-slave
who's devoted his life

627
01:03:59,480 --> 01:04:02,599
to the abolition of slavery,
and overcoming great obstacles

628
01:04:02,600 --> 01:04:06,039
and hardships along the
way, I should imagine.

629
01:04:06,040 --> 01:04:09,483
That's your story, isn't it?

630
01:04:18,560 --> 01:04:23,439
You and this young so-called lawyer
have proven you know what they are.

631
01:04:23,440 --> 01:04:26,120
They're Africans. Congratulations.

632
01:04:26,960 --> 01:04:29,879
What you don't know,
and, as far as I can tell,

633
01:04:29,880 --> 01:04:33,687
haven't bothered in the least
to discover is who they are.

634
01:04:37,760 --> 01:04:39,325
Right?

635
01:04:52,720 --> 01:04:54,684
Seven.

636
01:04:55,960 --> 01:04:57,844
Eight.

637
01:05:00,240 --> 01:05:01,441
Number nine.

638
01:05:02,480 --> 01:05:04,839
And 10.

639
01:05:04,840 --> 01:05:07,959
Now, how about you, Mr. Baldwin?

640
01:05:07,960 --> 01:05:10,599
Would you like to
count from one to 10?

641
01:05:10,600 --> 01:05:13,007
One to 10 in Mende.

642
01:05:14,840 --> 01:05:16,121
One.

643
01:05:16,680 --> 01:05:17,961
Two.

644
01:05:18,680 --> 01:05:20,041
Three.

645
01:05:20,920 --> 01:05:22,690
Four.

646
01:05:24,040 --> 01:05:26,481
Right. Five.

647
01:05:43,840 --> 01:05:48,125
Fresh fruit! Fresh fruit!
Straight from the Caribbean!

648
01:07:13,960 --> 01:07:15,959
What's happened here?

649
01:07:15,960 --> 01:07:17,959
I don't know. One of
them died last night.

650
01:07:17,960 --> 01:07:20,719
We tried to take the
body away to bury it.

651
01:07:20,720 --> 01:07:23,491
Well, what do they want?
They want to live with it?

652
01:07:39,640 --> 01:07:42,159
They want to bury him.

653
01:07:42,160 --> 01:07:45,159
They have to bury him according
to their Poro beliefs.

654
01:07:45,160 --> 01:07:47,601
Otherwise, his soul will
haunt them forever.

655
01:08:19,000 --> 01:08:22,479
If I were you, if I ran this place,

656
01:08:22,480 --> 01:08:26,605
I'd set protocol aside just this
once and let them bury him.

657
01:08:30,560 --> 01:08:33,684
I was thinking the same thing.

658
01:09:26,080 --> 01:09:29,039
My name is Kai Nyagua...

659
01:09:29,040 --> 01:09:31,288
My name is Kai Nyagua...
and James Covey.

660
01:09:31,400 --> 01:09:35,639
I speak Mende and English...
his language.

661
01:09:35,640 --> 01:09:40,846
You and he will talk to
each other through me.

662
01:09:42,880 --> 01:09:44,639
The clothes?

663
01:09:44,640 --> 01:09:46,719
And the names.

664
01:09:46,720 --> 01:09:49,839
I was rescued off a slave
ship by the British Navy.

665
01:09:49,840 --> 01:09:52,520
I never went back.

666
01:09:56,960 --> 01:09:58,844
A problem has arisen.

667
01:10:00,640 --> 01:10:03,439
The judge we had,
who believed, I believe,

668
01:10:03,440 --> 01:10:06,803
that you should be freed,
has been dismissed.

669
01:10:07,640 --> 01:10:11,439
A new judge has been called
upon to hear the case,

670
01:10:11,440 --> 01:10:13,210
this time without a jury.

671
01:10:23,520 --> 01:10:26,479
How is that possible?
A chief cannot be replaced.

672
01:10:26,480 --> 01:10:30,039
I can't explain it in any way that
you would understand, Cinque.

673
01:10:30,040 --> 01:10:34,290
Or me, for that matter. Only
that, well, it has happened.

674
01:10:40,680 --> 01:10:44,851
I am not a great orator
or advisor, Cinque.

675
01:10:45,720 --> 01:10:48,479
I'm not a big man in my profession.

676
01:10:48,480 --> 01:10:52,366
I don't know that I alone can convince
this next judge to set you free.

677
01:10:54,640 --> 01:10:56,490
I need your help.

678
01:10:58,680 --> 01:11:03,044
When we go to court,
I need you to speak.

679
01:11:10,280 --> 01:11:12,642
I'm not an advisor of any kind.

680
01:11:12,800 --> 01:11:15,119
I cannot speak for the others.

681
01:11:15,120 --> 01:11:17,519
Cinque, the others,
they say you can.

682
01:11:17,520 --> 01:11:21,088
They say you're the big man here.

683
01:11:23,240 --> 01:11:24,885
I am not.

684
01:11:30,960 --> 01:11:33,242
What's this I hear about a lion?

685
01:11:36,680 --> 01:11:39,919
They say you alone, alone, Cinque,

686
01:11:39,920 --> 01:11:43,999
slew the most terrifying
beast anyone has ever seen.

687
01:11:44,000 --> 01:11:46,089
Is it not true?

688
01:12:30,160 --> 01:12:32,681
It had killed several people.

689
01:12:35,120 --> 01:12:37,243
Even hunters among us.

690
01:12:38,200 --> 01:12:40,801
Everyone, including me, was afraid.

691
01:12:43,080 --> 01:12:47,569
I was lying down with my family
when out of nowhere it appeared.

692
01:12:49,200 --> 01:12:51,687
As it came for me,

693
01:12:52,280 --> 01:12:56,007
I picked up this big
rock and I threw it.

694
01:13:00,040 --> 01:13:04,040
And by some miracle,
you see, I hit it.

695
01:13:08,120 --> 01:13:10,402
He don't know how that
killed it, but it did.

696
01:13:15,480 --> 01:13:17,011
A rock.

697
01:13:20,520 --> 01:13:22,324
A rock.

698
01:13:42,960 --> 01:13:45,527
I received the gratitude of
everyone in the village.

699
01:13:51,840 --> 01:13:54,566
I was given respect.

700
01:13:57,240 --> 01:14:00,205
They treated me as
if I was a prince.

701
01:14:04,240 --> 01:14:07,410
They gave me the
fine country cloth.

702
01:14:17,000 --> 01:14:20,409
All these things they
gave me, I took them all.

703
01:14:25,280 --> 01:14:28,086
But I knew I didn't deserve it.

704
01:14:33,440 --> 01:14:36,399
For when I threw that
rock at that beast

705
01:14:36,400 --> 01:14:38,399
if I had missed him...

706
01:14:38,400 --> 01:14:39,719
He said he wouldn't
be sitting here today

707
01:14:39,720 --> 01:14:42,359
trying to explain these
things to you or me or...

708
01:14:42,360 --> 01:14:43,925
He'd just be dead.

709
01:14:49,080 --> 01:14:51,203
Understand?

710
01:14:56,880 --> 01:14:59,003
You understand?

711
01:15:13,720 --> 01:15:18,448
I'm not a big man,
just a lucky one.

712
01:15:28,280 --> 01:15:32,849
I might agree with you, Cinque,
except you're forgetting something.

713
01:15:37,800 --> 01:15:39,923
The other lion.

714
01:15:42,920 --> 01:15:45,559
The Amistad, Cinque.
The insurrection.

715
01:15:45,560 --> 01:15:48,809
That, too, was an accident?
I hardly think so.

716
01:15:55,600 --> 01:15:58,041
That wasn't bravery.

717
01:16:03,360 --> 01:16:06,040
Any man would do the same
to get back to his family.

718
01:16:07,320 --> 01:16:09,648
You yourself would do it.

719
01:16:20,360 --> 01:16:22,961
Someone said that might be yours.

720
01:16:28,760 --> 01:16:31,406
My wife gave it to me.

721
01:16:39,520 --> 01:16:41,324
To keep me safe.

722
01:16:42,760 --> 01:16:47,887
Cinque, I need you to
tell me how you got here.

723
01:17:21,120 --> 01:17:23,639
I'll hit you in the
head again if you talk.

724
01:17:23,640 --> 01:17:26,810
I'll share the profit of my harvest.
I'll give you all of it if you -

725
01:17:47,960 --> 01:17:52,404
Lomboko Slave Fortress

726
01:22:14,080 --> 01:22:18,251
This one's sick,
don't give him any.

727
01:22:26,680 --> 01:22:28,245
Her.

728
01:22:28,360 --> 01:22:30,244
Not her.

729
01:22:30,760 --> 01:22:32,325
Her.

730
01:24:06,280 --> 01:24:09,848
Havana, Cuba

731
01:25:31,120 --> 01:25:33,368
Bernardo.

732
01:26:09,800 --> 01:26:12,287
I wanted to kill them, too.

733
01:26:14,360 --> 01:26:18,565
For they convinced some of us that
they would take us back home.

734
01:26:25,080 --> 01:26:27,408
Thank you, sir.

735
01:26:34,280 --> 01:26:36,289
Mr. Holabird.

736
01:26:41,720 --> 01:26:43,799
Quite a tale.

737
01:26:43,800 --> 01:26:46,279
Intrigue, abduction,

738
01:26:46,280 --> 01:26:49,479
courage in the face of
unspeakable suffering.

739
01:26:49,480 --> 01:26:53,239
And all true. All right.
Now tell me if this is true.

740
01:26:53,240 --> 01:26:55,839
Certain tribes in Africa,
for hundreds of years,

741
01:26:55,840 --> 01:26:59,169
thousands, perhaps,
have owned slaves.

742
01:27:02,960 --> 01:27:05,128
Translate.

743
01:27:12,440 --> 01:27:13,679
Yes.

744
01:27:13,680 --> 01:27:16,679
And under what circumstances
might one become a slave,

745
01:27:16,680 --> 01:27:20,009
say, among the Mende,
of which you claim to belong?

746
01:27:21,640 --> 01:27:23,763
Translate.

747
01:27:32,160 --> 01:27:34,679
Wars, debts.

748
01:27:34,680 --> 01:27:36,199
I see.

749
01:27:36,200 --> 01:27:38,679
And how many men are
indebted to you?

750
01:27:38,680 --> 01:27:40,319
- I don't think you do see.
- Excuse me?

751
01:27:40,320 --> 01:27:41,999
- Your Honor, Mr. Holabird...
- It's different.

752
01:27:42,000 --> 01:27:43,679
Is trying to intimidate
my colleague.

753
01:27:43,680 --> 01:27:46,799
The Mende word for "slave"
is closer in meaning to "worker."

754
01:27:46,800 --> 01:27:49,359
Do these workers own
the land they work on?

755
01:27:49,360 --> 01:27:51,359
Do they receive wages?

756
01:27:51,360 --> 01:27:55,039
Are these workers free to not
work for you, if they so choose?

757
01:27:55,040 --> 01:27:57,039
Your Honor, he's questioning
the translator.

758
01:27:57,040 --> 01:27:59,439
No, the translator is answering
for the witness, Your Honor.

759
01:27:59,440 --> 01:28:02,199
- The witness isn't being given a chance.
- Mr. Baldwin!

760
01:28:02,200 --> 01:28:04,767
Fine, fine, Mr. Baldwin!

761
01:28:05,200 --> 01:28:08,039
Slavery, indentured servitude.

762
01:28:08,040 --> 01:28:10,279
Whatever they want to
call it, I don't mind.

763
01:28:10,280 --> 01:28:12,159
The concept is the same.

764
01:28:12,160 --> 01:28:15,364
Now, he is familiar
with the concept.

765
01:28:16,200 --> 01:28:19,199
After all, when you come down to it,
it's all about money, isn't it?

766
01:28:19,200 --> 01:28:22,839
Slaves, production, money.
I mean, that's the idea of it.

767
01:28:22,840 --> 01:28:26,010
Whether it's here or there.
I'm confused.

768
01:28:28,240 --> 01:28:33,399
Do your people routinely
slaughter their slaves

769
01:28:33,400 --> 01:28:36,843
in the manner that you just
so vividly described to us?

770
01:28:39,560 --> 01:28:43,799
Of course they don't. What
would be the point of that?

771
01:28:43,800 --> 01:28:47,399
I mean, killing your own slaves is
rather like burning down your own house

772
01:28:47,400 --> 01:28:49,682
or hut, isn't it?

773
01:28:52,000 --> 01:28:54,521
How do you explain that paradox?

774
01:29:00,600 --> 01:29:02,759
I don't understand what you mean.

775
01:29:02,760 --> 01:29:06,599
Sure you do. As does everyone here.

776
01:29:06,600 --> 01:29:09,439
The behavior you attribute
to your tormentors,

777
01:29:09,440 --> 01:29:11,439
your victims, to be more precise,

778
01:29:11,440 --> 01:29:14,919
and therefore every other
aspect of your testimony,

779
01:29:14,920 --> 01:29:18,329
makes no sense.

780
01:29:20,000 --> 01:29:22,119
Not even to you.

781
01:29:22,120 --> 01:29:23,599
But thank you for it.

782
01:29:23,600 --> 01:29:26,644
Like all good works of
fiction, it was entertaining.

783
01:29:26,960 --> 01:29:29,003
Nothing more.

784
01:29:39,480 --> 01:29:41,359
Captain Fitzgerald,
please explain to us

785
01:29:41,360 --> 01:29:44,279
your primary duties in
Her Majesty's navy.

786
01:29:44,280 --> 01:29:47,279
To patrol the Ivory
Coast for slave ships.

787
01:29:47,280 --> 01:29:49,319
Because?

788
01:29:49,320 --> 01:29:52,967
Because slavery is banned
in British law, sir.

789
01:29:53,280 --> 01:29:56,319
Yet the abduction of free men from the
British protectorate of Sierra Leone

790
01:29:56,320 --> 01:29:58,759
and their illegal transportation
to the New World,

791
01:29:58,760 --> 01:30:01,247
as described by Cinque,
is not unheard of, is it?

792
01:30:01,560 --> 01:30:04,411
Not even unusual, regrettably.

793
01:30:04,480 --> 01:30:09,811
Well, what, if anything, in his account
of his ordeal do you find believable?

794
01:30:11,160 --> 01:30:15,365
His description of the slave
fortress, for one thing.

795
01:30:15,960 --> 01:30:17,764
There is such a place.

796
01:30:17,880 --> 01:30:19,684
You've seen it?

797
01:30:20,160 --> 01:30:22,679
No, sir. We've not
managed to locate it,

798
01:30:22,680 --> 01:30:25,799
but there is overwhelming
evidence that it is real.

799
01:30:25,800 --> 01:30:28,287
What evidence, exactly? Rumor?

800
01:30:30,000 --> 01:30:31,999
Reports.

801
01:30:32,000 --> 01:30:36,683
By "reports" you mean of the variety
Cinque shared with us today?

802
01:30:37,480 --> 01:30:41,287
Its existence, sir,
has been reported.

803
01:30:41,400 --> 01:30:43,359
Cinque describes the
cold-blooded murder

804
01:30:43,360 --> 01:30:45,879
of a significant portion of the
people on board the Tecora.

805
01:30:45,880 --> 01:30:49,562
Mr. Holabird sees this as
a paradox. Do you, sir?

806
01:30:50,320 --> 01:30:55,359
Often when slavers are intercepted,
or believe they may be,

807
01:30:55,360 --> 01:30:57,759
they simply throw all
their prisoners overboard

808
01:30:57,760 --> 01:31:01,248
and thereby rid themselves of
the evidence of their crime.

809
01:31:01,920 --> 01:31:04,319
Drown hundreds of people?

810
01:31:04,320 --> 01:31:06,090
Yes.

811
01:31:06,480 --> 01:31:10,639
It hardly seems a lucrative
business to me, this slave-trading.

812
01:31:10,640 --> 01:31:12,599
Going to all that trouble
rounding everybody up

813
01:31:12,600 --> 01:31:14,211
only to throw them overboard.

814
01:31:14,360 --> 01:31:16,722
No, it's very lucrative.

815
01:31:17,320 --> 01:31:21,081
If only we could corroborate
Cinque's story somehow

816
01:31:23,240 --> 01:31:25,639
with evidence of some kind.

817
01:31:25,640 --> 01:31:27,888
The inventory.

818
01:31:32,320 --> 01:31:35,524
This? From the Tecora?

819
01:31:35,640 --> 01:31:39,319
If you look, there's a
notation made on May 10

820
01:31:39,320 --> 01:31:43,439
correcting the number of slaves on
board, reducing their number by 50.

821
01:31:43,440 --> 01:31:45,799
What does that mean?

822
01:31:45,800 --> 01:31:48,759
Well, if you look at it in
conjunction with Cinque's testimony,

823
01:31:48,760 --> 01:31:51,479
I would say that it means this.

824
01:31:51,480 --> 01:31:54,039
The Tecora crew, having
greatly underestimated

825
01:31:54,040 --> 01:31:57,319
the amount of provisions
required for their journey,

826
01:31:57,320 --> 01:32:00,808
solved the problem by
throwing 50 people overboard.

827
01:32:49,000 --> 01:32:51,639
I'm looking at the same
inventory, Captain,

828
01:32:51,640 --> 01:32:54,799
and I'm sorry, I don't
see where it says,

829
01:32:54,800 --> 01:32:57,999
"This morning, we threw
50 slaves overboard."

830
01:32:58,000 --> 01:33:00,043
On May 10 or any other day.

831
01:33:00,280 --> 01:33:02,528
As of course you would not.

832
01:33:16,040 --> 01:33:18,919
I do see that the
cargo weight changed.

833
01:33:18,920 --> 01:33:22,522
They reduced the poundage,
I see, but that is all.

834
01:33:38,400 --> 01:33:41,206
It's simple, ghastly arithmetic.

835
01:33:42,080 --> 01:33:44,999
Well, for you, perhaps.

836
01:33:45,000 --> 01:33:48,602
I may need a quill and parchment
and a better imagination.

837
01:33:48,920 --> 01:33:54,559
And what poundage do you imagine
the entry may refer to, sir?

838
01:33:54,560 --> 01:33:56,728
A mast and sails, perhaps?

839
01:33:57,160 --> 01:34:01,081
Give us, us free.

840
01:34:05,160 --> 01:34:09,524
Give us free.

841
01:34:12,080 --> 01:34:15,090
Give us, us free.

842
01:34:19,040 --> 01:34:23,484
Give us free.

843
01:34:24,200 --> 01:34:26,839
Your Honor, please
instruct the defendant

844
01:34:26,840 --> 01:34:29,599
that he cannot disrupt these
proceedings with such outbursts.

845
01:34:29,600 --> 01:34:33,168
Give us, us free!

846
01:34:33,240 --> 01:34:36,239
If we are to have any semblance of
order in this court, Your Honor...

847
01:34:36,240 --> 01:34:40,239
- Give us, us free! Give us, us free!
- He cannot keep crying out...

848
01:34:40,240 --> 01:34:43,159
Cannot keep crying out,
"Give us free,"

849
01:34:43,160 --> 01:34:45,279
- or anything else...
- Give us, us free!

850
01:34:45,280 --> 01:34:47,919
While I am trying to
question this witness!

851
01:34:47,920 --> 01:34:50,487
Give us, us free!

852
01:34:52,480 --> 01:34:55,411
Give us, us free!

853
01:35:55,760 --> 01:36:00,887
You don't have to pretend
to be interested in that.

854
01:36:01,160 --> 01:36:04,603
Nobody's watching but me.

855
01:36:04,640 --> 01:36:09,721
I'm not pretending. I'm
beginning to understand it.

856
01:36:24,760 --> 01:36:29,090
Their people have suffered more than ours...
Their lives were full of suffering.

857
01:36:31,520 --> 01:36:37,363
Then he was born and
everything changed.

858
01:36:45,240 --> 01:36:46,805
Who is he?

859
01:36:46,880 --> 01:36:53,770
I don't know, but everywhere he
goes, he is followed by the sun.

860
01:37:00,480 --> 01:37:04,446
Here he is healing
people with his hands.

861
01:37:10,040 --> 01:37:12,811
Protecting them...

862
01:37:17,560 --> 01:37:20,809
Being given children...

863
01:37:29,280 --> 01:37:31,289
What's this?

864
01:37:31,680 --> 01:37:35,009
He could also walk across the sea.

865
01:37:41,040 --> 01:37:43,686
But then something happened...

866
01:37:45,200 --> 01:37:49,564
He was captured.
Accused of some crime.

867
01:37:49,920 --> 01:37:54,409
Here he is with his hands tied.

868
01:37:57,120 --> 01:37:59,119
He must have done something.

869
01:37:59,120 --> 01:38:02,324
Why? What did we do?

870
01:38:02,640 --> 01:38:09,121
Whatever it was, it was serious
enough to kill him for it.

871
01:38:09,920 --> 01:38:13,681
Do you want to see
how they killed him?

872
01:38:21,480 --> 01:38:23,682
This is just a story, Yamba.

873
01:38:23,800 --> 01:38:29,040
But look. That's not the end of it.

874
01:38:31,080 --> 01:38:35,808
His people took his body down
from this... thing... this...

875
01:38:45,640 --> 01:38:52,485
They took him into a cave. They
wrapped him in a cloth, like we do.

876
01:38:52,840 --> 01:38:59,207
They thought he was dead, but he appeared
before his people again... and spoke to them.

877
01:39:01,600 --> 01:39:08,604
Then, finally,
he rose into the sky.

878
01:39:19,960 --> 01:39:25,690
This is where the soul
goes when you die here.

879
01:39:28,280 --> 01:39:33,122
This is where we're going
when they kill us.

880
01:39:37,480 --> 01:39:40,968
It doesn't look so bad.

881
01:40:23,640 --> 01:40:25,399
Buakei...

882
01:40:25,400 --> 01:40:27,250
Buakei... Hold your head up.

883
01:41:00,840 --> 01:41:02,804
All rise.

884
01:42:03,240 --> 01:42:07,081
After careful review and
thorough reflection,

885
01:42:08,760 --> 01:42:13,249
I find it impossible to deny the
power of the government's position.

886
01:42:15,360 --> 01:42:20,168
There is no doubt in my mind
that District Attorney Holabird,

887
01:42:20,880 --> 01:42:24,719
Her Catholic Majesty,
Isabella of Spain,

888
01:42:24,720 --> 01:42:27,879
and her trusted minister,
Señor Calderon,

889
01:42:27,880 --> 01:42:31,004
have all proceeded
with the utmost faith

890
01:42:31,880 --> 01:42:34,811
in the soundness of their case.

891
01:42:37,160 --> 01:42:42,159
I also believe that
Señors Ruiz and Montes

892
01:42:42,160 --> 01:42:45,799
may have misrepresented

893
01:42:45,800 --> 01:42:48,970
the origin of the prisoners.

894
01:42:50,120 --> 01:42:54,039
An issue which weighs
crucially upon their fate,

895
01:42:54,040 --> 01:42:56,846
and that of the Spaniards as well.

896
01:43:02,640 --> 01:43:05,923
Were they born in Africa?

897
01:43:13,400 --> 01:43:15,879
But since the answer to
that fundamental question

898
01:43:15,880 --> 01:43:18,319
shall so heavily govern every
determination of this court,

899
01:43:18,320 --> 01:43:20,204
I ask it again.

900
01:43:22,800 --> 01:43:25,651
Were they born in Africa?

901
01:43:39,520 --> 01:43:41,688
I believe they were.

902
01:43:47,960 --> 01:43:53,610
As such, Her Catholic Majesty's
claims of ownership have no merit.

903
01:43:55,560 --> 01:43:57,559
Neither, of course,
do those for salvage

904
01:43:57,560 --> 01:44:00,047
made by Lieutenants
Meade and Gedney.

905
01:44:00,200 --> 01:44:01,845
This is outrageous!

906
01:44:02,000 --> 01:44:07,604
I hereby order the immediate arrest and
detention of Señors Ruiz and Montes

907
01:44:10,720 --> 01:44:14,479
by federal marshals

908
01:44:14,480 --> 01:44:17,959
on the charge of slave-trading!

909
01:44:17,960 --> 01:44:20,128
Yes!

910
01:44:23,480 --> 01:44:25,839
The release of the Africans

911
01:44:25,840 --> 01:44:28,919
and their conveyance,
by this government

912
01:44:28,920 --> 01:44:31,519
at her earliest
convenience and expense,

913
01:44:31,520 --> 01:44:34,279
back to their homes in Africa!

914
01:44:34,280 --> 01:44:35,561
Yes!

915
01:44:44,880 --> 01:44:48,639
We've done it, Joadson!
We've done it! Yes!

916
01:44:48,640 --> 01:44:51,207
- Covey, tell them! Tell them now!
- I think they know.

917
01:44:58,240 --> 01:45:00,090
Look at 'em.

918
01:45:55,960 --> 01:45:58,719
What's most bewildering
to Her Majesty

919
01:45:58,720 --> 01:46:03,279
is this arrogant independence
of the American courts.

920
01:46:03,280 --> 01:46:07,239
After all, if you cannot rule
the courts, you cannot rule.

921
01:46:07,240 --> 01:46:09,919
Señor Calderon, as any true
American will tell you,

922
01:46:09,920 --> 01:46:13,567
it's the independence of our
courts that keeps us free.

923
01:46:22,240 --> 01:46:25,119
John, glad you're here.

924
01:46:25,120 --> 01:46:27,799
- Mr. Forsyth.
- The President...

925
01:46:27,800 --> 01:46:30,839
Mr. President?
Senator Calhoun is here.

926
01:46:30,840 --> 01:46:32,849
John!

927
01:46:33,440 --> 01:46:36,519
I was afraid you weren't
going to be able to join us.

928
01:46:36,520 --> 01:46:38,559
You may put that fear
to rest, Mr. President.

929
01:46:38,560 --> 01:46:40,479
Thank you. Please.

930
01:46:40,480 --> 01:46:44,002
I'd like you to meet Señor
Calderon, ambassador from Spain.

931
01:46:49,480 --> 01:46:51,119
I thought you said
he wasn't coming.

932
01:46:51,120 --> 01:46:53,243
He said he wasn't.

933
01:46:54,000 --> 01:46:56,919
You see, Señor Calderon,

934
01:46:56,920 --> 01:47:00,839
there's a growing number of people
in this part of the country

935
01:47:00,840 --> 01:47:04,039
that regard us in the South

936
01:47:04,040 --> 01:47:07,926
as not only geographically
beneath them.

937
01:47:09,360 --> 01:47:10,839
They ignore the fact

938
01:47:10,840 --> 01:47:15,319
that slavery is so interwoven
into the fabric of this society,

939
01:47:15,320 --> 01:47:19,923
that to destroy it would be
to destroy us as a people.

940
01:47:21,200 --> 01:47:24,722
It's immoral. That's all they know.

941
01:47:25,160 --> 01:47:28,045
Therefore, so are we.

942
01:47:28,840 --> 01:47:32,203
Immoral and inferior.

943
01:47:34,400 --> 01:47:37,763
We are inferior in one area.

944
01:47:38,240 --> 01:47:42,199
We're not as proficient
in the art of gain.

945
01:47:42,200 --> 01:47:46,239
We're not as wealthy as
our northern neighbors.

946
01:47:46,240 --> 01:47:49,479
We're still struggling.

947
01:47:49,480 --> 01:47:52,365
Take away our life's blood now...

948
01:47:54,560 --> 01:47:59,039
Well, we all know
what happens then,

949
01:47:59,040 --> 01:48:01,242
North and South.

950
01:48:02,640 --> 01:48:06,890
They become the masters,
and we the slaves.

951
01:48:08,200 --> 01:48:11,290
But not without a fight.

952
01:48:14,400 --> 01:48:17,079
Senator Calhoun is being modest.

953
01:48:17,080 --> 01:48:21,171
He's not inferior in another area.
The art of exaggeration.

954
01:48:25,600 --> 01:48:29,239
Ask yourself, Señor Calderon,

955
01:48:29,240 --> 01:48:34,401
what court wants to be responsible for
the spark that ignites the firestorm?

956
01:48:35,600 --> 01:48:39,999
What President wants
to be in office

957
01:48:40,000 --> 01:48:42,885
when it comes crashing
down around him?

958
01:48:45,280 --> 01:48:48,290
Certainly no court before this one.

959
01:48:49,960 --> 01:48:53,323
Certainly no President
before this one.

960
01:48:55,240 --> 01:48:56,646
So,

961
01:48:57,040 --> 01:49:00,005
judge us not too harshly, sir,

962
01:49:00,960 --> 01:49:04,959
and bid Her Majesty like,

963
01:49:04,960 --> 01:49:09,799
because the real determination our
courts and our President must make

964
01:49:09,800 --> 01:49:13,279
is not whether this
ragtag group of Africans

965
01:49:13,280 --> 01:49:16,199
raised swords against their enemy,

966
01:49:16,200 --> 01:49:19,768
but rather, must we?

967
01:49:47,840 --> 01:49:50,361
Come along, Mr. Joadson.

968
01:49:50,840 --> 01:49:53,361
This news...

969
01:49:53,680 --> 01:49:57,479
Well, of course, it's bad
news, but the truth is

970
01:49:57,480 --> 01:50:02,083
they may be more valuable to our
struggle in death than in life.

971
01:50:06,520 --> 01:50:09,519
Martyrdom, Mr. Joadson.

972
01:50:09,520 --> 01:50:14,521
From the dawn of Christianity, we have
seen no stronger power for change.

973
01:50:16,160 --> 01:50:17,964
Well, you know it's true.

974
01:50:20,720 --> 01:50:24,119
What is true, Mr. Tappan,

975
01:50:24,120 --> 01:50:28,119
and believe me when I
tell you I've seen this,

976
01:50:28,120 --> 01:50:30,759
is that there are some men
whose hatred of slavery

977
01:50:30,760 --> 01:50:34,679
is stronger than anything

978
01:50:34,680 --> 01:50:37,486
except for the slave himself.

979
01:50:40,400 --> 01:50:44,679
If you wish to inspire such
hatred in a man, Mr. Joadson,

980
01:50:44,680 --> 01:50:48,089
speak to him in that fashion
and it may come true.

981
01:51:19,280 --> 01:51:24,439
Our President, our big, big man,

982
01:51:24,440 --> 01:51:27,211
has appealed the decision
to our Supreme Court.

983
01:51:33,840 --> 01:51:36,008
What does that mean?

984
01:51:38,920 --> 01:51:41,885
We have to try the case again.

985
01:51:49,040 --> 01:51:51,839
Now, I know it's hard
to understand, Cinque.

986
01:51:51,840 --> 01:51:53,849
I don't understand,
for that matter.

987
01:51:57,160 --> 01:51:58,519
You said there would be a judgment,

988
01:51:58,520 --> 01:52:00,599
and if we won the judgment
we would go free!

989
01:52:00,600 --> 01:52:04,039
No, no. What I said is that
we won it at the state level.

990
01:52:04,040 --> 01:52:06,679
- No, no, no.
- That's what you said!

991
01:52:06,680 --> 01:52:10,885
What I said, that if we won it at
the state level, we then go on.

992
01:52:11,600 --> 01:52:13,211
That's what you said!

993
01:52:13,240 --> 01:52:16,639
- That's what you said.
- All right, yeah. Yes, I said it!

994
01:52:16,640 --> 01:52:20,970
I said it, but I shouldn't have.
What I should've said...

995
01:52:21,760 --> 01:52:23,519
- I can't translate that.
- You can't translate what?

996
01:52:23,520 --> 01:52:25,079
I can't translate "should."

997
01:52:25,080 --> 01:52:26,679
You saying there's no word
in Mende for "should"?

998
01:52:26,680 --> 01:52:29,559
No. Either you do something,
or you don't do it.

999
01:52:29,560 --> 01:52:32,679
What I meant to say...
What I meant to say?

1000
01:52:32,680 --> 01:52:35,360
Not in the way you mean it.

1001
01:52:38,880 --> 01:52:40,119
Try and understand me.

1002
01:52:40,120 --> 01:52:43,359
"Meant" is the same as "should."
You're misunderstanding the language.

1003
01:52:43,360 --> 01:52:45,359
Cinque, Cinque. Cinque!

1004
01:52:45,360 --> 01:52:48,159
Listen to me. Listen to me.
Understand what I'm saying.

1005
01:52:48,160 --> 01:52:52,126
What I said to you
before the judgment...

1006
01:52:52,200 --> 01:52:55,999
What I said to you before the
judgment is almost how it works here.

1007
01:52:56,000 --> 01:52:58,282
Almost.

1008
01:53:01,600 --> 01:53:03,199
Almost.

1009
01:53:03,200 --> 01:53:05,482
Yes, Cinque. But not always.

1010
01:53:07,960 --> 01:53:11,369
Yes. And that's what's happened.

1011
01:53:21,880 --> 01:53:24,765
What kind of place is this?

1012
01:53:29,680 --> 01:53:33,719
Where you almost mean what you say?
Where laws almost work?

1013
01:53:33,720 --> 01:53:37,561
How can you live like that?

1014
01:54:50,480 --> 01:54:53,799
"To His Excellency,
John Quincy Adams,"

1015
01:54:53,800 --> 01:54:57,759
"Massachusetts member,
House of Representatives."

1016
01:54:57,760 --> 01:55:00,319
"I have understood from Mr. Joadson"

1017
01:55:00,320 --> 01:55:04,639
"that you are acquainted with the
plight of the Amistad Africans."

1018
01:55:04,640 --> 01:55:07,919
"If that is true, then you
are aware that we have been,"

1019
01:55:07,920 --> 01:55:11,319
"at every step, successful in
our presentation of their case."

1020
01:55:11,320 --> 01:55:12,879
"Yet despite this,"

1021
01:55:12,880 --> 01:55:16,439
"and despite the unlikelihood of
President Van Buren's re-election,"

1022
01:55:16,440 --> 01:55:19,679
"he has appealed our most
recent favorable decision"

1023
01:55:19,680 --> 01:55:21,962
"to the highest court in the land."

1024
01:55:22,600 --> 01:55:25,079
"As I'm sure you are well aware,"

1025
01:55:25,080 --> 01:55:27,319
"seven of nine of these
Supreme Court justices"

1026
01:55:27,320 --> 01:55:30,569
"are themselves
Southern slave owners."

1027
01:55:38,560 --> 01:55:41,679
"Sir, we need you."

1028
01:55:41,680 --> 01:55:45,679
"If ever there was a time for a man
to cast aside his daily trappings"

1029
01:55:45,680 --> 01:55:48,839
"and array himself for
battle, that time has come."

1030
01:55:48,840 --> 01:55:51,566
Thank you.

1031
01:55:51,640 --> 01:55:55,599
"Cicero once said, appealing to
Claudius in defense of the Republic,"

1032
01:55:55,600 --> 01:55:57,519
"that the whole result
of this entire war"

1033
01:55:57,520 --> 01:56:00,999
"depends on the life of one
most brave and excellent man."

1034
01:56:01,000 --> 01:56:05,359
"In our time, in this instance,
I believe it depends on two."

1035
01:56:05,360 --> 01:56:09,199
"A courageous man, at present in
irons in New Haven, named Cinque,"

1036
01:56:09,200 --> 01:56:10,970
"and you, sir."

1037
01:56:18,560 --> 01:56:21,206
"Sincerely, Roger S. Baldwin,
attorney-at-law."

1038
01:56:31,680 --> 01:56:34,804
Mr. Tippings, excuse
me a moment, please.

1039
01:56:41,920 --> 01:56:44,600
- Any word from Mr. Adams?
- What did Cinque say?

1040
01:56:45,320 --> 01:56:47,879
He won't talk to you.

1041
01:56:47,880 --> 01:56:50,319
He won't talk to me?

1042
01:56:50,320 --> 01:56:51,760
No.

1043
01:56:56,320 --> 01:56:58,319
How's your English coming?

1044
01:56:58,320 --> 01:57:00,966
No better than my Mende, I suppose.

1045
01:57:02,320 --> 01:57:04,479
You know, Cinque,
I realize this isn't something

1046
01:57:04,480 --> 01:57:06,759
you necessarily want
to think about,

1047
01:57:06,760 --> 01:57:10,931
but has it occurred to you
that I'm all you've got?

1048
01:57:11,600 --> 01:57:13,119
Because as it happens,

1049
01:57:13,120 --> 01:57:16,279
since my practice has completely
deteriorated in the past months,

1050
01:57:16,280 --> 01:57:18,528
you're all I've got.

1051
01:57:28,120 --> 01:57:31,005
See, this is me. You see?

1052
01:57:31,480 --> 01:57:33,489
You see? You see how this works?

1053
01:57:33,680 --> 01:57:39,410
And this here, Cinque, is for me.

1054
01:57:41,640 --> 01:57:43,001
More death threats.

1055
01:57:43,880 --> 01:57:47,527
Some of them signed.
By my own clients, no less.

1056
01:57:48,240 --> 01:57:51,250
I should say former
clients, shouldn't I?

1057
01:57:57,480 --> 01:58:02,208
There is one more consequence to
having no clientele to speak of.

1058
01:58:04,760 --> 01:58:06,291
I am now free

1059
01:58:06,440 --> 01:58:10,919
to sit here as long as it takes
for you to acknowledge me.

1060
01:58:10,920 --> 01:58:15,045
Yes, you understood that
word, didn't you? "Free."

1061
01:58:16,240 --> 01:58:18,363
All right.

1062
01:58:22,000 --> 01:58:24,487
Then we'll just sit.

1063
01:58:27,320 --> 01:58:29,204
Caesar.

1064
01:58:31,560 --> 01:58:34,759
Cicero's appeal was to
Julius Caesar, not Claudius.

1065
01:58:34,760 --> 01:58:38,119
Claudius would not be born
for another hundred years.

1066
01:58:38,120 --> 01:58:40,926
You were right, there
was one of them.

1067
01:58:42,080 --> 01:58:43,519
Is that him?

1068
01:58:43,520 --> 01:58:45,245
Yes, Mr. President.

1069
01:58:46,560 --> 01:58:48,279
Please unlock this door.

1070
01:58:48,280 --> 01:58:51,199
Adams has flirted with the
abolitionists for 15 years,

1071
01:58:51,200 --> 01:58:52,999
but has yet to take one
home from the dance.

1072
01:58:53,000 --> 01:58:55,039
How old is he anyway?

1073
01:58:55,040 --> 01:58:58,199
Old enough to know he's too old to
take anyone home from the dance.

1074
01:58:58,200 --> 01:59:00,799
I hear he sleeps through three-quarters
of the sessions on the Hill.

1075
01:59:00,800 --> 01:59:04,039
Let's see. President, slumbering
congressman from Massachusetts,

1076
01:59:04,040 --> 01:59:08,450
jail-house lawyer, one waits with
great anticipation for what's next.

1077
01:59:08,960 --> 01:59:11,679
- Well, what must that be like?
- What?

1078
01:59:11,680 --> 01:59:15,159
Knowing all your life,
whatever your accomplishments,

1079
01:59:15,160 --> 01:59:18,079
you'll only be remembered as
the son of a great father.

1080
01:59:18,080 --> 01:59:20,559
The only thing John Quincy
Adams will be remembered for

1081
01:59:20,560 --> 01:59:22,250
is his middle name.

1082
01:59:25,240 --> 01:59:29,490
I wonder, is there anything as
pathetic as an ex-President?

1083
01:59:35,640 --> 01:59:38,366
I was talking about John...

1084
02:00:06,560 --> 02:00:09,119
- Sir.
- Yes?

1085
02:00:09,120 --> 02:00:13,039
Cinque has asked me to ask you

1086
02:00:13,040 --> 02:00:16,279
whether you have thought about
the question of jurisdiction.

1087
02:00:16,280 --> 02:00:17,519
What?

1088
02:00:17,520 --> 02:00:22,169
That since they took over
the ship far out to sea,

1089
02:00:23,040 --> 02:00:26,359
and since neither Spain
nor America owns the sea,

1090
02:00:26,360 --> 02:00:29,166
how is it that the treaty applies?

1091
02:00:31,360 --> 02:00:35,167
Tell him the treaty recognizes
no jurisdictional limitations.

1092
02:00:37,040 --> 02:00:40,279
- Well?
- He will ask me why.

1093
02:00:40,280 --> 02:00:43,245
Because I said so.

1094
02:00:55,160 --> 02:00:57,159
Excuse me, sir.

1095
02:00:57,160 --> 02:00:58,839
Yes?

1096
02:00:58,840 --> 02:01:01,119
Cinque would like to know

1097
02:01:01,120 --> 02:01:04,439
that if he is the legal
property of Ruiz and Montes,

1098
02:01:04,440 --> 02:01:08,879
then how does the treaty apply, since
it is between America and Spain?

1099
02:01:08,880 --> 02:01:11,839
Or their citizens.

1100
02:01:11,840 --> 02:01:16,719
"Or their citizens" is included
in the language, if he must know.

1101
02:01:16,720 --> 02:01:19,048
Thank you, sir.

1102
02:01:22,280 --> 02:01:25,211
It's a good point, though.

1103
02:01:32,280 --> 02:01:35,039
Does Great Britain have any
treaties with West Africa

1104
02:01:35,040 --> 02:01:37,319
which may override those
between Spain and America?

1105
02:01:37,320 --> 02:01:38,719
No.

1106
02:01:38,720 --> 02:01:40,679
Does Great Britain have
any treaties with America

1107
02:01:40,680 --> 02:01:42,639
which might override those
between Spain and...

1108
02:01:42,640 --> 02:01:44,159
No.

1109
02:01:44,160 --> 02:01:46,359
Does the American government have
any treaties with West Africa?

1110
02:01:46,360 --> 02:01:47,399
No.

1111
02:01:47,400 --> 02:01:49,039
Does Spain have any
treaties with West Africa?

1112
02:01:49,040 --> 02:01:50,399
No!

1113
02:01:50,400 --> 02:01:52,879
Does the Commonwealth of Connecticut
have any treaties with West Africa?

1114
02:01:52,880 --> 02:01:56,766
No, no, no, no! Now stop this!

1115
02:02:02,400 --> 02:02:04,959
Unshackle him.

1116
02:02:04,960 --> 02:02:07,439
I'm sorry, sir. I'm under
strict orders to ensure the...

1117
02:02:07,440 --> 02:02:10,120
Unshackle him, please.

1118
02:02:12,560 --> 02:02:15,445
Yes, Mr. President.

1119
02:02:23,320 --> 02:02:28,560
This is a Phalaenopsis, moth
orchid, I brought over from China.

1120
02:02:29,720 --> 02:02:33,049
And this is a primrose
from an English garden.

1121
02:02:34,600 --> 02:02:39,761
And this spear lily, from
the south of France.

1122
02:02:42,720 --> 02:02:45,599
This is my rose, Blush Noisette.

1123
02:02:45,600 --> 02:02:49,319
This came all the way
from Washington DC,

1124
02:02:49,320 --> 02:02:52,285
but don't tell anyone.

1125
02:03:05,360 --> 02:03:07,642
Go on, go on.

1126
02:03:22,640 --> 02:03:26,970
African violet. I can't tell you
how difficult that was to come by.

1127
02:03:40,800 --> 02:03:45,210
Now, you understand you're
going to the Supreme Court.

1128
02:03:46,640 --> 02:03:48,410
Do you know why?

1129
02:03:56,000 --> 02:03:58,407
It is the place where
they finally kill us.

1130
02:03:58,720 --> 02:04:01,924
No. Well, yes, that
may be true, too.

1131
02:04:03,600 --> 02:04:05,559
That's not what I meant.

1132
02:04:05,560 --> 02:04:08,799
No, there is another reason
and a more important reason.

1133
02:04:08,800 --> 02:04:13,927
Although, I'll admit that
perhaps more so to us than you.

1134
02:04:14,800 --> 02:04:18,079
All right, don't... Yeah.

1135
02:04:18,080 --> 02:04:20,282
Cinque.

1136
02:04:22,120 --> 02:04:27,247
Do you know who I am?
Has anyone told you about me?

1137
02:04:31,880 --> 02:04:33,199
Yes.

1138
02:04:33,200 --> 02:04:34,845
What have they told you?

1139
02:04:39,400 --> 02:04:41,039
That you are a chief.

1140
02:04:41,040 --> 02:04:43,242
I was a chief, yes.

1141
02:04:49,640 --> 02:04:53,447
A chief cannot become anything
less than a chief, even in death.

1142
02:04:55,200 --> 02:04:58,879
How I wish such were
true here, Cinque.

1143
02:04:58,880 --> 02:05:01,162
You've no idea.

1144
02:05:02,680 --> 02:05:06,679
One tries to govern
wisely, strongly, but...

1145
02:05:06,680 --> 02:05:10,646
One tries to govern in a way that
betters the lives of one's villagers.

1146
02:05:13,080 --> 02:05:16,443
One tries to kill the lion.

1147
02:05:19,520 --> 02:05:24,647
Unfortunately, one isn't always
wise enough or strong enough.

1148
02:05:27,280 --> 02:05:29,448
Time passes

1149
02:05:30,960 --> 02:05:33,288
and the moment is gone.

1150
02:05:35,920 --> 02:05:39,639
Now, listen, Cinque. Listen.

1151
02:05:39,640 --> 02:05:44,839
We're about to bring your case

1152
02:05:44,840 --> 02:05:47,679
before the highest
court in our land.

1153
02:05:47,680 --> 02:05:50,639
We're about to do
battle with a lion

1154
02:05:50,640 --> 02:05:54,322
that is threatening to
rip our country in two.

1155
02:05:56,080 --> 02:06:00,888
And all we have on
our side is a rock.

1156
02:06:03,720 --> 02:06:07,322
Of course, you didn't
ask to be at the center

1157
02:06:07,640 --> 02:06:09,679
of this historic conflagration
any more than I did,

1158
02:06:09,680 --> 02:06:12,039
but we find ourselves
here nonetheless

1159
02:06:12,040 --> 02:06:15,919
by some mysterious
mix of circumstances

1160
02:06:15,920 --> 02:06:18,919
and all the world watching.

1161
02:06:18,920 --> 02:06:22,283
So, what are we to do?

1162
02:06:26,480 --> 02:06:32,722
Is he going to help? He has far
many more questions than answers.

1163
02:06:33,080 --> 02:06:36,799
- What'd he just say?
- Sorry. I didn't catch it.

1164
02:06:36,800 --> 02:06:41,399
Cinque, look. I'm being
honest with you.

1165
02:06:41,400 --> 02:06:44,410
Anything less would
be disrespectful.

1166
02:06:46,160 --> 02:06:49,839
I'm telling you, I'm preparing you,

1167
02:06:49,840 --> 02:06:53,719
I suppose I'm explaining to you

1168
02:06:53,720 --> 02:06:57,242
that the test ahead of us is an
exceptionally difficult one.

1169
02:07:03,440 --> 02:07:05,165
We won't be going in there alone.

1170
02:07:05,400 --> 02:07:07,799
Alone? Indeed not. No.
We have right at our side.

1171
02:07:07,800 --> 02:07:10,039
We have righteousness at our side.

1172
02:07:10,040 --> 02:07:12,447
We have Mr. Baldwin over there.

1173
02:07:19,720 --> 02:07:22,526
I meant my ancestors.

1174
02:07:26,720 --> 02:07:28,604
I will call into the past,

1175
02:07:30,560 --> 02:07:32,762
far back to the beginning of time,

1176
02:07:33,720 --> 02:07:36,400
and beg them to come and help me

1177
02:07:38,120 --> 02:07:39,731
at the judgment.

1178
02:07:40,960 --> 02:07:44,289
I will reach back and
draw them into me.

1179
02:07:46,400 --> 02:07:48,364
And they must come,

1180
02:07:49,360 --> 02:07:53,884
for at this moment, I am the whole
reason they have existed at all.

1181
02:08:31,040 --> 02:08:32,446
Thank you.

1182
02:08:36,240 --> 02:08:38,727
Your Honors,

1183
02:08:40,640 --> 02:08:42,679
I derive much consolation

1184
02:08:42,680 --> 02:08:47,169
from the fact that my colleague,
Mr. Baldwin here,

1185
02:08:48,920 --> 02:08:51,999
has argued the case

1186
02:08:52,000 --> 02:08:55,886
in so able and so complete a manner

1187
02:08:57,920 --> 02:09:01,841
as to leave me scarcely
anything to say.

1188
02:09:02,680 --> 02:09:04,928
However,

1189
02:09:06,600 --> 02:09:09,167
why are we here?

1190
02:09:10,200 --> 02:09:14,079
How is it that a simple,
plain property issue

1191
02:09:14,080 --> 02:09:17,839
should now find itself so
ennobled as to be argued before

1192
02:09:17,840 --> 02:09:20,999
the Supreme Court of the
United States of America?

1193
02:09:21,000 --> 02:09:23,959
I mean, do we fear the lower
courts, which found for us easily,

1194
02:09:23,960 --> 02:09:25,679
somehow missed the truth?
Is that it?

1195
02:09:25,680 --> 02:09:30,599
Or is it, rather, our great and
consuming fear of civil war

1196
02:09:30,600 --> 02:09:32,759
that has allowed us
to heap symbolism

1197
02:09:32,760 --> 02:09:35,519
upon a simple case that
never asked for it?

1198
02:09:35,520 --> 02:09:38,559
And now would have
us disregard truth

1199
02:09:38,560 --> 02:09:42,401
even as it stands before us,
tall and proud as a mountain.

1200
02:09:43,600 --> 02:09:45,959
The truth,

1201
02:09:45,960 --> 02:09:51,564
in truth, has been driven
from this case like a slave,

1202
02:09:51,920 --> 02:09:56,011
flogged from court to court,
wretched and destitute.

1203
02:09:57,000 --> 02:10:01,279
And not by any great legal acumen

1204
02:10:01,280 --> 02:10:04,324
on the part of the
opposition, I might add.

1205
02:10:05,720 --> 02:10:10,164
But through the long, powerful
arm of the executive office.

1206
02:10:11,240 --> 02:10:13,959
Yea, this is no mere property case.
Gentlemen, I put it to you thus.

1207
02:10:13,960 --> 02:10:17,559
This is the most important case
ever to come before this court.

1208
02:10:17,560 --> 02:10:21,765
Because what it in fact concerns

1209
02:10:23,080 --> 02:10:25,760
is the very nature of man.

1210
02:10:29,280 --> 02:10:31,289
These are...

1211
02:10:35,800 --> 02:10:37,679
These are transcriptions of letters

1212
02:10:37,680 --> 02:10:43,079
written between our Secretary
of State, John Forsyth,

1213
02:10:43,080 --> 02:10:46,124
and the Queen of
Spain, Isabella II.

1214
02:10:47,600 --> 02:10:50,599
Now, I ask that you

1215
02:10:50,600 --> 02:10:54,122
accept their perusal as
part of your deliberations.

1216
02:10:54,440 --> 02:10:55,926
Thank you, sir.

1217
02:10:57,880 --> 02:11:00,559
I would not touch on them
now except to notice

1218
02:11:00,560 --> 02:11:04,003
a curious phrase which
is much repeated.

1219
02:11:05,280 --> 02:11:09,963
The Queen again and again refers
to our "incompetent courts."

1220
02:11:11,160 --> 02:11:15,331
Now what, I wonder,
would be more to her liking?

1221
02:11:16,440 --> 02:11:19,883
A court that finds
against the Africans?

1222
02:11:20,280 --> 02:11:22,448
Well, I think not.

1223
02:11:23,800 --> 02:11:27,199
And here is the fine point of it.

1224
02:11:27,200 --> 02:11:31,799
What Her Majesty wants is a court
that behaves just like her courts,

1225
02:11:31,800 --> 02:11:35,639
the courts this 11-year-old
child plays with

1226
02:11:35,640 --> 02:11:38,799
in her magical kingdom
called Spain.

1227
02:11:38,800 --> 02:11:40,879
A court that will
do what it is told.

1228
02:11:40,880 --> 02:11:44,766
A court that can be
toyed with like a doll.

1229
02:11:45,640 --> 02:11:50,562
A court, as it happens,
of which our own President,

1230
02:11:52,000 --> 02:11:54,479
Martin Van Buren,
would be most proud.

1231
02:11:54,480 --> 02:11:56,011
Thank you.

1232
02:11:56,800 --> 02:12:00,399
This is a publication of the
office of the President.

1233
02:12:00,400 --> 02:12:05,527
It's called The Executive Review,
and I'm sure you all read it.

1234
02:12:06,240 --> 02:12:09,999
At least I'm sure the President
hopes you all read it.

1235
02:12:10,000 --> 02:12:14,959
This is a recent issue,
and there's an article in here

1236
02:12:14,960 --> 02:12:19,609
written by "a keen mind
of the South," who...

1237
02:12:20,280 --> 02:12:23,879
My former Vice President,
John Calhoun, perhaps.

1238
02:12:23,880 --> 02:12:25,605
Could it be?

1239
02:12:26,080 --> 02:12:29,079
Who asserts that "there has never
existed a civilized society"

1240
02:12:29,080 --> 02:12:34,366
"in which one segment did not
thrive upon the labor of another."

1241
02:12:35,000 --> 02:12:37,239
"As far back as one
chooses to look,"

1242
02:12:37,240 --> 02:12:40,239
"to ancient times,
to biblical times,"

1243
02:12:40,240 --> 02:12:42,920
"history bears this out."

1244
02:12:43,280 --> 02:12:47,119
"In Eden, where only
two were created,"

1245
02:12:47,120 --> 02:12:51,689
"even there, one was pronounced
subordinate to the other."

1246
02:12:52,640 --> 02:12:57,926
"Slavery has always been with us
and is neither sinful nor immoral."

1247
02:13:00,320 --> 02:13:05,559
"Rather, as war and antagonism
are the natural states of man,"

1248
02:13:05,560 --> 02:13:10,448
"so, too, slavery,
as natural as it is inevitable."

1249
02:13:13,000 --> 02:13:17,119
Well, gentlemen, I must say I differ
with the keen minds of the South

1250
02:13:17,120 --> 02:13:19,919
and with our President, who
apparently shares their views,

1251
02:13:19,920 --> 02:13:22,919
offering that the natural
state of mankind is instead,

1252
02:13:22,920 --> 02:13:26,639
and I know this is a
controversial idea,

1253
02:13:26,640 --> 02:13:29,002
is freedom.

1254
02:13:31,440 --> 02:13:33,608
Is freedom.

1255
02:13:35,120 --> 02:13:37,479
And the proof is the
length to which a man,

1256
02:13:37,480 --> 02:13:40,160
woman or child will go to
regain it, once taken.

1257
02:13:40,760 --> 02:13:43,599
He will break loose his chains.

1258
02:13:43,600 --> 02:13:46,559
He will decimate his enemies.

1259
02:13:46,560 --> 02:13:49,919
He will try and try and try,

1260
02:13:49,920 --> 02:13:54,599
against all odds,
against all prejudices,

1261
02:13:54,600 --> 02:13:56,723
to get home.

1262
02:14:00,080 --> 02:14:04,171
Cinque, would you stand
up, if you would,

1263
02:14:05,160 --> 02:14:07,840
so everyone can see you.

1264
02:14:10,600 --> 02:14:14,486
This man is black.
We can all see that.

1265
02:14:15,520 --> 02:14:20,089
But can we also see as easily
that which is equally true?

1266
02:14:20,400 --> 02:14:24,446
That he is the only
true hero in this room.

1267
02:14:25,160 --> 02:14:28,279
Now, if he were white, he wouldn't
be standing before this court,

1268
02:14:28,280 --> 02:14:29,919
fighting for his life.

1269
02:14:29,920 --> 02:14:32,159
If he were white and his
enslavers were British,

1270
02:14:32,160 --> 02:14:34,159
he wouldn't be able to stand,

1271
02:14:34,160 --> 02:14:36,079
so heavy the weight of
the medals and honors

1272
02:14:36,080 --> 02:14:38,487
we would bestow upon him.

1273
02:14:39,000 --> 02:14:42,319
Songs would be written about him.

1274
02:14:42,320 --> 02:14:45,808
The great authors of our times
would fill books about him.

1275
02:14:45,920 --> 02:14:48,079
His story would be told

1276
02:14:48,080 --> 02:14:51,887
and retold in our classrooms.

1277
02:14:53,600 --> 02:14:58,039
Our children, because we
would make sure of it,

1278
02:14:58,040 --> 02:15:01,926
would know his name as well
as they know Patrick Henry's.

1279
02:15:03,440 --> 02:15:05,879
Yet, if the South is right,

1280
02:15:05,880 --> 02:15:10,369
what are we to do with that
embarrassing, annoying document,

1281
02:15:10,760 --> 02:15:13,759
the Declaration of Independence?

1282
02:15:13,760 --> 02:15:16,804
What of its conceits?

1283
02:15:17,880 --> 02:15:20,359
"All men created equal,"
"inalienable rights,"

1284
02:15:20,360 --> 02:15:23,564
"life, liberty," and
so on and so forth?

1285
02:15:25,440 --> 02:15:28,199
What on Earth are
we to do with this?

1286
02:15:28,200 --> 02:15:31,006
I have a modest suggestion.

1287
02:15:50,800 --> 02:15:55,279
The other night I was talking
with my friend Cinque.

1288
02:15:55,280 --> 02:15:59,451
He was over at my place, and we were
out in the greenhouse together.

1289
02:15:59,800 --> 02:16:04,439
And he was explaining to me how
when a member of the Mende,

1290
02:16:04,440 --> 02:16:07,119
that's his people...

1291
02:16:07,120 --> 02:16:09,839
How when a member of the
Mende encounters a situation

1292
02:16:09,840 --> 02:16:13,439
where there appears no hope at all,

1293
02:16:13,440 --> 02:16:16,610
he invokes his ancestors.

1294
02:16:17,120 --> 02:16:19,448
Tradition.

1295
02:16:22,920 --> 02:16:26,959
See, the Mende believe that if one can
summon the spirit of one's ancestors,

1296
02:16:26,960 --> 02:16:30,519
then they have never left,

1297
02:16:30,520 --> 02:16:35,039
and the wisdom and strength
they fathered and inspired

1298
02:16:35,040 --> 02:16:37,641
will come to his aid.

1299
02:16:45,240 --> 02:16:49,199
James Madison, Alexander Hamilton,

1300
02:16:49,200 --> 02:16:51,959
Benjamin Franklin,

1301
02:16:51,960 --> 02:16:54,162
Thomas Jefferson,

1302
02:16:55,960 --> 02:16:58,128
George Washington,

1303
02:17:01,280 --> 02:17:03,403
John Adams.

1304
02:17:11,560 --> 02:17:15,239
We have long resisted
asking you for guidance.

1305
02:17:15,240 --> 02:17:18,159
Perhaps we have feared in doing so,

1306
02:17:18,160 --> 02:17:20,719
we might acknowledge
that our individuality,

1307
02:17:20,720 --> 02:17:25,369
which we so, so revere,
is not entirely our own.

1308
02:17:27,000 --> 02:17:29,359
Perhaps we've feared

1309
02:17:29,360 --> 02:17:32,530
an appeal to you might
be taken for weakness.

1310
02:17:34,000 --> 02:17:39,525
But we've come to understand,
finally, that this is not so.

1311
02:17:41,160 --> 02:17:44,479
We understand now,

1312
02:17:44,480 --> 02:17:48,079
we've been made to understand

1313
02:17:48,080 --> 02:17:51,568
and to embrace the understanding

1314
02:17:54,000 --> 02:18:00,128
that who we are is who we were.

1315
02:18:09,120 --> 02:18:11,999
We desperately need your
strength and wisdom

1316
02:18:12,000 --> 02:18:17,286
to triumph over our fears,
our prejudices, ourselves.

1317
02:18:18,040 --> 02:18:21,519
Give us the courage
to do what is right.

1318
02:18:21,520 --> 02:18:25,645
And if it means civil
war, then let it come.

1319
02:18:27,120 --> 02:18:29,439
And when it does,

1320
02:18:29,440 --> 02:18:31,919
may it be, finally,

1321
02:18:31,920 --> 02:18:35,249
the last battle of the
American Revolution.

1322
02:18:43,880 --> 02:18:46,447
That's all I have to say.

1323
02:19:31,840 --> 02:19:37,839
In the case of The United States of
America v. The Amistad Africans,

1324
02:19:37,840 --> 02:19:40,799
it is the opinion of this court

1325
02:19:40,800 --> 02:19:45,399
that our treaty of 1795 with Spain,

1326
02:19:45,400 --> 02:19:50,119
on which the prosecution has
primarily based its arguments,

1327
02:19:50,120 --> 02:19:52,879
is inapplicable.

1328
02:19:52,880 --> 02:19:56,319
While it is clearly
stipulated in Article 9

1329
02:19:56,320 --> 02:20:00,959
that, and I quote,
seized ships and cargo

1330
02:20:00,960 --> 02:20:04,846
are to be returned entirely
to their proprietary,

1331
02:20:05,320 --> 02:20:06,759
the end of quote,

1332
02:20:06,760 --> 02:20:09,884
it has not been shown to
the court's satisfaction

1333
02:20:10,400 --> 02:20:14,400
that these particular Africans
fit that description.

1334
02:20:15,440 --> 02:20:18,279
We are then left with
the alternative,

1335
02:20:18,280 --> 02:20:21,159
that they are not slaves

1336
02:20:21,160 --> 02:20:24,728
and therefore cannot be
considered merchandise

1337
02:20:25,720 --> 02:20:29,639
but are, rather, free individuals

1338
02:20:29,640 --> 02:20:33,159
with certain legal
and moral rights,

1339
02:20:33,160 --> 02:20:36,959
including the right to
engage in insurrection

1340
02:20:36,960 --> 02:20:41,519
against those who would
deny them their freedom.

1341
02:20:41,520 --> 02:20:45,199
And therefore, over one dissent,

1342
02:20:45,200 --> 02:20:46,970
it is the court's judgment

1343
02:20:47,200 --> 02:20:51,530
that the defendants are to be
released from custody at once

1344
02:20:52,400 --> 02:20:54,919
and, if they so choose,

1345
02:20:54,920 --> 02:20:58,920
to be returned to their
homes in Africa.

1346
02:21:16,120 --> 02:21:18,119
Good job.

1347
02:21:18,120 --> 02:21:20,129
Thank you.

1348
02:21:26,560 --> 02:21:27,921
Well...

1349
02:21:33,320 --> 02:21:36,126
What did you say to them?

1350
02:21:39,360 --> 02:21:42,928
What words did you use
to persuade them?

1351
02:21:44,760 --> 02:21:46,291
Yours.

1352
02:22:29,120 --> 02:22:31,766
To keep you safe.

1353
02:23:07,160 --> 02:23:12,924
Thank you, Baldwin.

1354
02:23:57,720 --> 02:24:02,687
The Liberation of
Lomboko Slave Fortress

1355
02:24:06,400 --> 02:24:08,090
Fire!

1356
02:24:09,360 --> 02:24:12,769
Let's get a move on! Lively now!

1357
02:24:42,240 --> 02:24:44,519
All clear, sir.

1358
02:24:44,520 --> 02:24:46,484
- Fire.
- Fire!

1359
02:24:50,920 --> 02:24:52,770
- Fire.
- Fire!

1360
02:24:57,520 --> 02:24:59,210
Fire.

1361
02:25:02,240 --> 02:25:03,441
Fire.

1362
02:25:04,680 --> 02:25:06,006
Fire.

1363
02:25:12,640 --> 02:25:15,977
William Henry Harrison
defeated Martin Van Buren to

1364
02:25:16,003 --> 02:25:19,146
become the ninth President
of the United States.

1365
02:25:24,000 --> 02:25:25,850
Take a letter, Ensign.

1366
02:25:26,680 --> 02:25:31,439
To His Honor, the United States
Secretary of State, Mr. John Forsyth.

1367
02:25:31,440 --> 02:25:33,319
"My dear Mr. Forsyth,"

1368
02:25:33,320 --> 02:25:37,969
"it is my great pleasure to inform
you that you are, in fact, correct."

1369
02:25:38,320 --> 02:25:42,969
"The slave fortress in Sierra
Leone does not exist."

1370
02:26:45,800 --> 02:26:48,860
Queen Isabella of Spain
continued to argue the

1371
02:26:48,886 --> 02:26:52,271
Amistad matter with seven
more American Presidents.

1372
02:27:00,120 --> 02:27:03,682
In 1864, her hopes of
"compensation" finally collapsed

1373
02:27:03,708 --> 02:27:06,751
with the Confederate
Army's defeat at Atlanta.

1374
02:27:10,400 --> 02:27:16,767
Cinque returned to Sierra Leone to find
his own people engaged in civil war.

1375
02:27:17,320 --> 02:27:21,605
His village was destroyed
and his family gone.

1376
02:27:22,280 --> 02:27:26,690
It is believed they
were sold into slavery.

1377
02:27:27,000 --> 02:27:30,128
Best watched using Open Subtitles MKV Player
